WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:13.510
Tim Ferriss: Good afternoon, this is Edward
Murrow.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:18.529
Ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:22.080
This is Tim Ferriss.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.779
Welcome to another episode of The Tim Ferriss
Show, ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:31.170
I have one of my favorite people in front
of me, Kevin Kelly.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:32.210
Who's Kevin Kelly?

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.770
Kevin Kelly helped launch and edit Wired magazine.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.980
He has written for The New York Times, and
The Wall Street Journal, among many other

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.160
publications.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.340
He's the author of the new book Excellent
Advice for Living: Wisdom I Wish I'd Known

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:44.340
Earlier.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.710
I have a lot to say about this book.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:46.710
We will get into it.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:50.930
Other books by Kevin Kelly include Out of
Control, the 1994 classic book on decentralized

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:56.510
emergent systems; The Silver Cord, a graphic
novel about robots and angels; What Technology

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:01.390
Wants, a robust theory of technology; Vanishing
Asia, his 50-year project to photograph the

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:06.240
disappearing cultures of Asia, and The Inevitable:
Understanding the 12 Technological Forces

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.180
That Will Shape Our Future, a New York Times
bestseller.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:13.950
Kevin is currently co-chair of The Long Now
Foundation, which is building a clock in a

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:17.790
mountain that will tick for 10,000 years,
of course.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:22.100
He also has a daily blog, a weekly podcast
about cool tools and a weekly newsletter,

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:27.270
Recomendo, a free, one-page list of six very
brief recommendations of cool stuff.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.180
You can find that at recomendo.com.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.409
That is R-E-C-O-M-E-N-D-O.com.

00:01:30.409 --> 00:01:36.860
I was going to say R-E-C-O-M-E-N-DO.com.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.110
You get the idea.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:40.130
So recomendo.com.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:41.130
Take a look.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:44.280
He is also a senior maverick at Wired and
lives in Pacifica, California.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:50.170
You can find him on Twitter @Kevin2Kelly,
Kevin, the number two, Kelly, and all things

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.000
Kevin at kk.org.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.460
Kevin, nice to see you again.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.060
Kevin Kelly: Tim, it's always a pleasure and
just seeing you makes me happy.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.060
Tim Ferriss: Likewise.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.060
Kevin Kelly: So glad to be here.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.000
Tim Ferriss: Hey, I saw you walk in.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.710
I was chatting with Harley of Shopify earlier
today, and you walked in, and I saw you across

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:11.000
the room with your yellow baseball cap on.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.330
Doesn't hide the beard, though.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.510
So, I spotted you and also just made me very
happy to see you.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:21.220
I was thinking about that and how incredibly
valuable that is.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.220
What a gift.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:23.220
Kevin Kelly: It is.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.890
And I'm glad to be seen.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:32.900
And so I'm so glad to be able to share with
you another time of exploring some ideas and

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:33.989
just see where they go.

00:02:33.989 --> 00:02:43.110
Tim Ferriss: Now, ideas, you're a man of ideas,
and I thought for comic relief, we might start

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:47.239
with a list of possible topics to discuss
with Kevin Kelly.

00:02:47.239 --> 00:02:52.670
So, I just want to read these, because people
who are perhaps not long-term listeners may

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:55.580
not have heard our previous conversations.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:03:03.760
And I believe the title I used, you did not
choose this, I chose it, for our first conversation,

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.170
was "Kevin Kelly, the Real-Life Most Interesting
Man in the World."

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:09.870
Something along those lines.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.190
People may say, "What hyperbole.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:12.580
What is this nonsense?"

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:20.159
But wait, allow me to list the possible topics,
and I ask every guest to send possible bullets

00:03:20.159 --> 00:03:21.159
for exploration.

00:03:21.159 --> 00:03:22.159
So, here we go.

00:03:22.159 --> 00:03:24.140
And then I will return to a few of these.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:28.210
"The most popular thing I've ever written:
1,000 True Fans." "2008: why we built a clock

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:30.330
that will tick for 10,000 years inside a mountain."

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:34.209
"I've had a daily blog for 20 years, for five
years a weekly podcast."

00:03:34.209 --> 00:03:36.819
"At Wired, we invented the click-on advertising
banner for the web.

00:03:36.819 --> 00:03:41.349
Next, I spent 11 years creating a huge graphic
novel about angels and robots, released on

00:03:41.349 --> 00:03:42.470
Kickstarter, The Silver Cord."

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:43.470
We're going to come back to that.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.670
"My failed campaign to discover all the species
of life on Earth."

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:52.240
"In 2003, I made a long bet on the collapse
of the global human population by 2060."

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:55.540
"My TED Talk on why we should be optimistic
in 10 minutes."

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.240
"The most important article I've ever written."

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.709
"My case against belief in an AI singularity."

00:04:00.709 --> 00:04:04.900
"My most recent piece in Wired extolling the
glories of generative AI engines of wow."

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.930
"I co-founded The Hackers Conference in 1984,
still going."

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:11.650
"My 50-year passion project," and this goes
on, "weighing 30 pounds," about Vanishing

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:12.650
Asia.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:17.520
"I rode my bike across America twice, once
west to east, once north to south."

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:19.150
"I make a piece of art every day."

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:23.350
"My biggest audience, and most of my fans
are in China, where I'm known simply as KK."

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:27.600
"I have a screen credit for working with Spielberg
on the sci-fi concepts for Minority Report."

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.120
"With a friend, I built a two-family house
from scratch, cutting down the trees."

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.840
It goes on and on and on, right?

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:36.410
"The story of my religious conversion on This
American Life in 1997."

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:43.289
"I made a music video in 1969, 12 years before
MTV."

00:04:43.289 --> 00:04:44.660
Lest people think I'm exaggerating —
Kevin Kelly: It sounds ridiculous now that

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:45.680
you mention it.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.390
Tim Ferriss: It does sound ridiculous, and
thank God I'm a specialist in the ridiculous.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:54.740
So I thought we would start with this bet.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:05:01.780
"In 2003, I made a long bet on the collapse
of the global human population by 2060."

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:06.169
What is a long bet and which direction did
you bet?

00:05:06.169 --> 00:05:07.450
Were you betting on or against?

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:08.450
Kevin Kelly: Sure.

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:09.450
Tim Ferriss: And why?

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:16.110
Kevin Kelly: So Long Bets is a service that
we set up at the Long Now Foundation, which

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:21.020
I can explain a little bit more about that,
which is meant to encourage long-term thinking.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:26.361
So we made a place where we could have a long
bet, meaning more than two years, about some

00:05:26.361 --> 00:05:29.940
socially significant wager.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.640
And the idea would be that there would be
a public bet and you'd be accountable for

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:37.139
it, and there'd be money involved in the wager.

00:05:37.139 --> 00:05:45.139
And the idea was to also require people to
put the logic of why they were going in a

00:05:45.139 --> 00:05:46.639
certain direction.

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:51.120
And that, over time, if you had enough of
this, you could see which kinds of logic and

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:55.220
what kinds of thinking would win more often.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:06:00.940
And to get around the laws of betting at the
time, which basically is illegal to make a

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:02.970
bet, that's been kind of slowly changing.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:08.210
But we engineered a kind of a hacker, which
you could make a donation to.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:13.810
Well, you would use the money and the money
would go to the foundation, the nonprofit

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:15.600
of the person who won the bet.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.389
So my bet, and by the way, there are a lot
of people who bet on, including Warren Buffett,

00:06:20.389 --> 00:06:26.800
made a million-dollar bet that basically index
funds would beat any investment hedge fund.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:27.930
He won.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:30.759
Well, again, his charity won.

00:06:30.759 --> 00:06:38.060
My bet about the population was that the population
of the world, the global population of the

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:42.180
world, by 2060, I think it was —
Tim Ferriss: 2060.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:50.250
Kevin Kelly: — would be the same as it was
at the time of the bet, which is, I think,

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:51.250
2003.

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:56.610
So the idea is that we are coming up to a
peak of human population that would then on

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:57.750
the other side go down.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:03.100
So you very commonly see the chart of the
rising population.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:06.630
But it's interesting to me that you never
see what happens on the other side.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:11.180
And what happens on the other side as far
as we can tell, is that it plummets.

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:13.349
And that's because —
Tim Ferriss: Resource scarcity?

00:07:13.349 --> 00:07:14.419
Kevin Kelly: No.

00:07:14.419 --> 00:07:16.539
Tim Ferriss: Education and falling birth rates?

00:07:16.539 --> 00:07:22.590
Kevin Kelly: Just falling birth rates because
modern people, on average, are not having

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:25.120
more than two kids per couple.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:26.280
And this is common.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:32.500
So fertility is falling all around the planet,
including right now, even in the US.

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:38.840
And everything that we've tried, we being
humans, have tried collectively to counter

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:40.169
that, has not worked.

00:07:40.169 --> 00:07:45.680
So Japan is famously losing the total number
of people, not just having a lower birth rate.

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:50.210
They actually have a decline in population,
but they're actually not the lowest birth

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:53.560
rate, which is South Korea.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:58.110
And China is aging, Mexico is aging faster
than the US.

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:02.220
So all the people that have been coming from
Mexico, Mexico will want to have come back

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:03.560
at some point.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:08.139
So it's a really significant change.

00:08:08.139 --> 00:08:12.980
And again, it's possible that we could use
technology to change it, maybe have artificial

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:14.630
wombs or who knows what.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:19.871
But right now, for the average person, they're
not inclined to have a lot of children.

00:08:19.871 --> 00:08:23.700
And the people who do have children don't
have enough to cover for those who don't,

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:26.540
in terms of the world population.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:32.010
You can have immigration, which is what the
US has been doing all along, basically stealing

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.380
people from other countries.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:35.630
But that doesn't help you globally.

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:37.320
So here's where it's the problem.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:41.539
For a lot of environmentalists, this is good
news because there's less people who are —

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:42.539
Tim Ferriss: Consuming.

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:45.170
Kevin Kelly: Consuming resources.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:53.060
But throughout history, we've always only
had rising living standards with rising population.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:54.060
Tim Ferriss: Really?

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:55.060
Kevin Kelly: Yes.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:58.360
We have no experience —
Tim Ferriss: Do you think those are causal

00:08:58.360 --> 00:08:59.360
or correlated?

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:01.510
Kevin Kelly: That's the question.

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:06.170
I think there's obviously some feedback loop
where the more people you have, the more ideas

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:11.480
you have, the more wealth you have, and that
allows you to have more kids.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:13.020
But we don't know.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:18.910
So, all we can say is we have no experience
in having living standards with a smaller

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:23.020
population, a smaller audience, smaller market
every year.

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:27.019
Tim Ferriss: What do you think are the implications
of this?

00:09:27.019 --> 00:09:35.120
And is 2060 just after the apex?

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:37.660
Will it have been declining for a period of
time?

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:40.760
When is the projected apex, if you have a
projection?

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:41.760
Kevin Kelly: Right.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.779
So that's one of the evidences is that this
peak is keeps moving closer.

00:09:46.779 --> 00:09:47.779
Okay.

00:09:47.779 --> 00:09:48.779
Because —
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:09:48.779 --> 00:09:49.779
It's not static.

00:09:49.779 --> 00:09:53.089
Kevin Kelly: Well, it's not static, but it
also means that there's all the projections

00:09:53.089 --> 00:09:57.290
about the increase in population that people
are assuming and built into some of these

00:09:57.290 --> 00:09:59.480
demographic models are being revised all the
time.

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:01.510
So that peak keeps moving closer.

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:06.370
And the height of the peak, the numbers of
actually how high it is, is changing.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:11.519
I think one of the things that's really important
to understand for us in our society right

00:10:11.519 --> 00:10:19.290
now is that if you ask any question at the
global level, the answer is, "We have no idea."

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:22.560
And the one thing we know most about is human
population.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:28.120
And I think our number or counting of that
is probably off by 10 percent, plus or minus.

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:30.510
And that's the thing that we know the most
about globally.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:32.970
But if you ask how much fresh water is there?

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:35.770
How much electricity is being generated?

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:38.810
Globally, the answer is is that we really
don't know.

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:45.510
We have a very poor view of us globally, partly
because there's areas of the world that are

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:47.480
so undeveloped, we don't have very good countings
—

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:50.800
Tim Ferriss: I see poor view meaning we just
have very incomplete understanding.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:51.800
Kevin Kelly: Yes.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:56.060
We don't have a global census, we don't have
a global way of viewing.

00:10:56.060 --> 00:11:02.190
We have now satellites that can help us see,
but they can't count everything.

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:07.600
And so I think what we're doing as a species
is moving into this era where we'll become

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:08.600
a global.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:12.930
We have a global economy, we have a global
view, a global machine.

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:17.750
All the internets connected together and we'll
act more globally and maybe increasingly some

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:22.100
global governance, but we're not there yet.

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:28.840
And so even something as primal and essential,
as foundational, as our population, I think

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:29.840
we don't even, don't know.

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:33.570
Tim Ferriss: Have you had any exposure to
or interaction with the Santa Fe Institute?

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:34.570
I have not.

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:41.290
But I've recently had conversations with a
number of people, Bill Gurley and others who

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:44.380
are involved, complex adaptive systems.

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:48.860
And I'd just be curious to know what your
exposure has been, what your opinion is.

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:52.769
Kevin Kelly: So my first book was written
about basically the Santa Fe Institute.

00:11:52.769 --> 00:11:55.940
Out Of Control was based from a conference
that was initiated by a conference I went

00:11:55.940 --> 00:12:02.590
to at Los Alamos, and Santa Fe was hosting
one of the first conferences.

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:06.029
I would go down there and talk to all the
scientists.

00:12:06.029 --> 00:12:09.269
And this is in the late '80s.

00:12:09.269 --> 00:12:13.850
And that was the beginning of the sort of
complex adaptive system view of the world.

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:20.459
And so that's what Out Of Control is about,
is looking at that view and saying the way

00:12:20.459 --> 00:12:23.490
biology works and the way this complex technology
works are very similar.

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:27.350
They have very similar dynamics because they're
complex adaptive systems.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:33.630
What we want to make with the internet, with
all its penetrations, that you can think of

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:38.829
spam as an invading virus that you have to
— you can't eliminate, but you have to treat

00:12:38.829 --> 00:12:42.100
it like an immune system where you keep it
at bay.

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:48.630
And so adopting some of these biological dynamics
and applying it to machines and a lot of the

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:54.220
work in trying to make robots and early AI,
again, we're modeling off of what was being

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:59.800
learned and often reported or sponsored by
the Santa Fe Institute and that kind of approach

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:01.440
to complex adaptive systems.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:04.080
So, yes, I think it's incredibly important.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:09.329
And for me, it was a transformative framing
of the complicated things was to think of

00:13:09.329 --> 00:13:11.389
them in these terms.

00:13:11.389 --> 00:13:17.079
My whole book was about the fact that the
world of the made and the world of the born

00:13:17.079 --> 00:13:23.170
are basically the same, two faces of the same
kind of dynamic.

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:28.230
And so you could look at how meadows work
in ecosystems, and then you could look at

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:34.170
the internet, which was just beginning, and
now of course, it's in full bloom.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:37.200
And you can see how social media, they have
similar behaviors.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:38.220
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:43.339
And you can find thing in one that you then
find in the other in surprising ways, sort

00:13:43.339 --> 00:13:46.600
of life imitating art and art imitating life,
in the sense that we think we have invented

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:47.600
gears.

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:49.090
And then we are like, "Oh, wait a second.

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:52.899
Actually, there are insects that use gears
for types of jumping."

00:13:52.899 --> 00:13:53.899
How wild is that?

00:13:53.899 --> 00:13:54.899
Kevin Kelly: Right.

00:13:54.899 --> 00:13:59.870
So, there's biomimicry, that was the field,
which was kind of using those as models for

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:02.829
ideas and frameworks for trying to make mechanical
things.

00:14:02.829 --> 00:14:04.959
And that only takes you so far.

00:14:04.959 --> 00:14:11.460
I mean that was the, maybe the genius or the
breakthrough in the Wright Brothers, which

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:14.910
is like, we didn't make flying machines by
flapping —

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:16.520
Tim Ferriss: Strapping wings to people.

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:20.399
Kevin Kelly: But by flapping our wings, it
was like you put a big surfboard on it and

00:14:20.399 --> 00:14:21.399
you fly.

00:14:21.399 --> 00:14:25.890
So, there's limits to it, and right now with
the AI stuff, there's lots of looking at the

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:29.269
neurology and of course we call them neuronets.

00:14:29.269 --> 00:14:32.230
So there is huge amount of influence.

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:37.889
But what I'm saying is even maybe a little
stronger, which is that it isn't as if these

00:14:37.889 --> 00:14:42.720
mechanical systems are imitating biology.

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:45.899
I'm saying they actually have the same dynamics.

00:14:45.899 --> 00:14:52.149
The dynamics that are powering biology are
powering the technium and the technology.

00:14:52.149 --> 00:14:53.149
It's the same.

00:14:53.149 --> 00:14:59.190
Tim Ferriss: I think we are quite close on
that in the sense that both paths end up in

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:01.300
the same places.

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:02.750
And I would tend to agree.

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:06.160
Los Alamos, is there any particular reason
they chose Los Alamos?

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:11.550
Kevin Kelly: So, Los Alamos, there were a
lot of physicists left over from the Manhattan

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:14.490
Project who liked living there.

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:17.949
And Murray Gell-Mann was the prime mover.

00:15:17.949 --> 00:15:19.121
And so he liked it.

00:15:19.121 --> 00:15:21.900
And I think he might have been instrumental
in finding the funding for it, and he was

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:24.420
on the chair for a very long time.

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:28.510
And they had spaces to convene and people,
so Santa Fe was close to Los Alamos, and that

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:30.110
was the reason why it was in Santa Fe.

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:31.110
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:33.910
I've been revisiting some of Richard Feynman's
writing.

00:15:33.910 --> 00:15:36.089
Kevin Kelly: And he might have been part of
that whole thing too.

00:15:36.089 --> 00:15:37.319
I mean he loved —
Tim Ferriss: He was there, for sure.

00:15:37.319 --> 00:15:38.569
Kevin Kelly: I mean, in terms of [inaudible]
Santa Fe.

00:15:38.569 --> 00:15:40.160
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I see, that group.

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:41.160
That I don't know.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:44.460
But certainly that is part of the reason I
asked about Los Alamos.

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:45.460
It's on the brain.

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:52.079
So let's take a hard left, which is I think
going to be common in this conversation.

00:15:52.079 --> 00:15:55.380
And we will probably come back, we'll almost
certainly come back to AI, but I don't want

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:56.420
to open that can of worms just yet.

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:57.420
Kevin Kelly: Right, right.

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:58.420
Exactly.

00:15:58.420 --> 00:15:59.420
Tim Ferriss: All right.

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:02.470
You spent 11 years creating a huge graphic
novel about angels and robots.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:03.730
Silver Cord.

00:16:03.730 --> 00:16:09.370
I am, you may or may not know this, but deeply
interested in comic books and collected for

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:15.660
a very long time, spent all of my allowance,
all of my work money, almost all of it, on

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:18.560
comics for a very long period of time, wanted
to be a penciller.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:19.990
Why did it take 11 years?

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:26.560
And what did the process of translating your
thinking and writing to that form look like?

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:28.000
What were the steps?

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:36.089
Kevin Kelly: I met a friend who actually was
actually comic book artist, had published,

00:16:36.089 --> 00:16:38.880
and wanted to do another one.

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:44.920
And I'd kind of always wanted to try my hand
at it because I thought that this was a brilliant

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:47.040
genre for communicating lots of things.

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:51.040
And particularly if you're interested in science
fiction, it was sort of like to me a little

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:54.550
better than a novel because it had that kind
of immersive visualization, which I love.

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:59.370
I'm a very visual person, but it wasn't so
detailed that you needed to make a movie of

00:16:59.370 --> 00:17:00.370
it.

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:01.370
But we thought we could do both.

00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:06.120
And we thought that maybe we could write something
that would have some appeal, be making movies.

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:09.650
So we would try to write it, the script, as
if we were writing a movie.

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:17.060
And so some of the other associates that we
had, worked for Pixar, some story later.

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:18.980
Tim Ferriss: You say associates, it sounds
like a law firm.

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:19.980
What do you mean?

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:21.439
Kevin Kelly: Well, I mean —
Tim Ferriss: Collaborators.

00:17:21.439 --> 00:17:22.439
Friends.

00:17:22.439 --> 00:17:23.439
Kevin Kelly: Actually, here's what it was.

00:17:23.439 --> 00:17:25.510
My friend and these other friends all went
to the same church.

00:17:25.510 --> 00:17:29.220
So we were all in the same church, and there
were people who worked at Pixar and some people

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:30.940
who worked at ILM.

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:38.059
And so I had this idea of doing this book
or this story, and the story was, the premise

00:17:38.059 --> 00:17:43.950
of it was I was imagining that there would
be these interdimensional beings.

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:45.160
We're calling them angels.

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:50.270
They're made out of light because they're
intangible, and that they would look down

00:17:50.270 --> 00:17:57.780
on humans and weep when they saw us because
we were getting the ride that they craved,

00:17:57.780 --> 00:17:59.919
that embodiment, and we were squandering it.

00:17:59.919 --> 00:18:06.510
So that's the basic premise, is that there
is this realm and there's these beings and

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:11.900
they're waiting their turn to be embodied,
and they're looking at us and what we're doing

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:13.340
with — it's like, "You have the ultimate…"

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:14.650
Tim Ferriss: You're blowing it, guys.

00:18:14.650 --> 00:18:16.230
Kevin Kelly: And you're blowing it.

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:18.010
It's like, what would I do?

00:18:18.010 --> 00:18:24.130
I would smother my face with mango juice and
I would take a dive into the ocean and swim

00:18:24.130 --> 00:18:26.460
underwater, the whole — so that was the
premise of it.

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:33.570
And then the added part of it was that some
of these angelic beings would try to cheat

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:36.710
by becoming embodied into robots.

00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:44.720
They wouldn't go through the traditional preparation
that you require of moral guidance and whoever

00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:47.390
else you needed to be before you were allowed
to be in a human.

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:49.320
But they were going to cheat by coming into
robots.

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:50.320
Tim Ferriss: Skipping a few steps.

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:54.480
Kevin Kelly: Skipping a few steps, and these
would be kind of unhinged or rogue because

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:55.480
they weren't.

00:18:55.480 --> 00:18:56.630
So, anyway, that's the premise.

00:18:56.630 --> 00:19:01.350
So you had these angels and robots and it
was a graphic novel, and we would tell stories.

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:06.620
And the issue was I'd never written fiction
in my life, although the Pixar people had.

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:12.580
They were from the story side, and I couldn't
draw to the level necessary.

00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:13.761
So, we worked on it.

00:19:13.761 --> 00:19:18.409
And the reason why it took 11 years was we
made it way too big.

00:19:18.409 --> 00:19:21.440
Instead of doing it like little 20-page things.

00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:24.330
Tim Ferriss: Decided to do —
Kevin Kelly: Decided the whole thing.

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:25.330
300.

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:27.140
Tim Ferriss: Lord of the Rings in one go.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:28.179
Kevin Kelly: Exactly.

00:19:28.179 --> 00:19:33.250
No, actually, we got a advance from Simon
&amp; Schuster to do it, and we were late in delivering,

00:19:33.250 --> 00:19:35.030
and the guy who bought it left.

00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:37.110
They wanted it back, blah, blah, blah.

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:38.470
Tim Ferriss: Occupational hazard in publishing.

00:19:38.470 --> 00:19:39.470
Kevin Kelly: Exactly right.

00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:44.000
So it took us that long just to finish, and
we actually Kickstarted it to print it.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:51.300
Tim Ferriss: When you were generating the
story, were you doing it in effectively screenplay

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:52.300
form?

00:19:52.300 --> 00:19:53.300
Kevin Kelly: Yes.

00:19:53.300 --> 00:19:54.300
It was written as a screenplay script.

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:55.980
Tim Ferriss: And what did you hope?

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:58.930
What did you collectively, and maybe it was
different person-to-person, hope this story

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:00.630
would do, if anything?

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:04.580
Kevin Kelly: It's a really good question,
and that's the most important question you

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:07.400
always want to be asking yourself when you're
doing these.

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:09.300
What effect do you want to have on people?

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:11.600
How do you want them to feel after they're
done?

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:13.710
Do you want them to change their behavior?

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:21.679
And for me, it was this idea of the genesis
of it, which was to nudge people a little

00:20:21.679 --> 00:20:32.040
bit more, to take advantage of this special
time that we have to interact with each other.

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:34.730
This is what you get by being embodied, is
that there's far more influence.

00:20:34.730 --> 00:20:41.010
We can influence things through the physical
way that we can't when we're intangible beings.

00:20:41.010 --> 00:20:46.890
And that was the issue that these other dimensional
beings had, is that they don't have as much

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:47.890
influence.

00:20:47.890 --> 00:20:52.049
It's really hard for them to influence because
having a body means that you can influence

00:20:52.049 --> 00:20:54.100
things by interacting with them physically.

00:20:54.100 --> 00:20:55.100
And that's very powerful.

00:20:55.100 --> 00:20:56.150
Tim Ferriss: And experience things.

00:20:56.150 --> 00:20:58.270
Kevin Kelly: And experience things, right.

00:20:58.270 --> 00:21:00.919
And so that's what it was.

00:21:00.919 --> 00:21:09.780
It was an ode or a nudge for people to maybe
appreciate their own lives, meaning literally

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:12.130
their life, much more than they do.

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:13.919
Tim Ferriss: I dig it, Kevin Kelly.

00:21:13.919 --> 00:21:20.779
You're good at helping friends, myself included,
to do that IRL, in real life too, through

00:21:20.779 --> 00:21:27.809
experiences, and we may come back to that,
but first, the iconic “1,000 True Fans.”

00:21:27.809 --> 00:21:30.400
The most popular thing you've ever written.

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:37.890
Why do you think that is the case, and what
would you double-down on or revise if you

00:21:37.890 --> 00:21:43.380
were to take another stab at it today?

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:47.080
Kevin Kelly: So, the honest answer, one of
the reasons why it's very — we can talk

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:50.190
about maybe why it's good and useful and then
why it's popular.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:53.720
The reason why it's popular is actually through
you.

00:21:53.720 --> 00:22:00.789
The fact that you included it in one of your
books, and that's sort of lifted it out of

00:22:00.789 --> 00:22:02.640
my little realm.

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:09.039
The reason why maybe it kind of resonates
with people is because there is sort of an

00:22:09.039 --> 00:22:18.429
assumption that the goal is to hit it big,
the big-time bestseller, a hit.

00:22:18.429 --> 00:22:24.460
And most people kind of come to associate
those numbers, that kind of large scale, with

00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:25.460
success.

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:33.340
And the idea that success could look differently,
that you could have a more modest-size scale

00:22:33.340 --> 00:22:38.470
and that be successful, sometimes is dismissed
as lifestyle businesses or whatever.

00:22:38.470 --> 00:22:45.211
And I kind of realized that technology would
allow a different version of that.

00:22:45.211 --> 00:22:49.170
You could have it, that it was possible and
that it would be good.

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:51.690
It would be good for people.

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:57.880
And so that was, I think, people can resonate
with that because it's a viable alternative

00:22:57.880 --> 00:23:01.570
option to things that was not spoken before.

00:23:01.570 --> 00:23:04.230
It was not even really on the radar.

00:23:04.230 --> 00:23:09.370
And when I wrote it, when I first wrote it,
before you even saw it, there was no Kickstarter,

00:23:09.370 --> 00:23:10.730
there was no Patreon.

00:23:10.730 --> 00:23:16.279
And I was challenged with people like Jaron
Lanier to say, "Well, that's a nice theory,

00:23:16.279 --> 00:23:19.809
but there isn't any evidence that this is
actually working."

00:23:19.809 --> 00:23:24.860
It was actually at that time, I did a follow
through and I tried to find evidence, and

00:23:24.860 --> 00:23:30.470
there was evidence of established artists
from publishing, or music, or studios who

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:35.700
had already an audience and could move off
of that to their own, But there wasn't any

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:41.140
evidence of an indigenous organic growth from
nothing.

00:23:41.140 --> 00:23:42.140
Now there is.

00:23:42.140 --> 00:23:48.470
Every day people write to me, and meet me,
and say, "Yes, I have been able to do that,"

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:50.650
inspired someone by hearing of that possibility.

00:23:50.650 --> 00:23:57.680
Tim Ferriss: Is there anything that you would
modify in that piece, or emphasize more?

00:23:57.680 --> 00:24:04.030
Kevin Kelly: I did a modification for you
where I talked about the fact that, again,

00:24:04.030 --> 00:24:06.220
one benefit and one disadvantage.

00:24:06.220 --> 00:24:13.650
The one benefit is that part of what we're
doing is, if all you need is 1,000 true fans,

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:22.190
then even if your interests are one in a million,
given the population of the Earth of billions

00:24:22.190 --> 00:24:27.020
of people, that means that there's 1,000 people
who potentially, on the planet, who will share

00:24:27.020 --> 00:24:28.020
your interests.

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:32.429
So if your interests are only one in a million
people can identify with, that you still have

00:24:32.429 --> 00:24:33.429
enough.

00:24:33.429 --> 00:24:40.789
Then, the second thing was that, just to emphasize
to people, this is not for everybody, that

00:24:40.789 --> 00:24:46.420
tending the fans and interacting with them
is almost like a halftime job, at least, maybe

00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:47.640
even more.

00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:49.250
Not everybody's suited to do that.

00:24:49.250 --> 00:24:52.640
An artist might just want to paint, they don't
want to deal with fans.

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:58.659
We see more of dealing with fans, what it
means, is it's not always pretty, and it can

00:24:58.659 --> 00:24:59.659
burn you out.

00:24:59.659 --> 00:25:04.049
So I just want to emphasize that this is an
option.

00:25:04.049 --> 00:25:05.870
Secondly, you don't have to go all the way.

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:10.919
You can have your thousand true fans and then
you can have lots of other casual fans and

00:25:10.919 --> 00:25:15.059
other fans which would allow you to have other
people help you.

00:25:15.059 --> 00:25:16.700
So it's not just you.

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:21.230
Then, secondly, for some people you want to
have intermediaries.

00:25:21.230 --> 00:25:25.679
It's just not something you want to spend
your time doing and that's perfectly fine,

00:25:25.679 --> 00:25:29.529
but it's a really great place to be able to
start from.

00:25:29.529 --> 00:25:35.250
Maybe you don't want to land there, but that's
one of my pieces of advice, is that where

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:38.760
you start is not where you're going to land,
and so this is a good place to start.

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:44.940
Tim Ferriss: That's exactly what I was going
to say, which is even if you want to hypothetically

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:49.630
build a huge company and change the world,
although I'm very skeptical of people who

00:25:49.630 --> 00:25:55.260
lead with that, I think most businesses fundamentally
are lifestyle businesses.

00:25:55.260 --> 00:25:58.770
If you really double-click and look at it
closely enough, even if someone aims to be

00:25:58.770 --> 00:26:00.409
a Fortune 500 CEO.

00:26:00.409 --> 00:26:07.250
In any case, the point I want to make is even
if you have these very lofty large scale goals,

00:26:07.250 --> 00:26:15.470
beginning with the exercise of reading “1,000
True Fans,” and at least considering what

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:19.970
your approach would be to accomplish that
first, is a great fundamental step.

00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:21.289
Kevin Kelly: Right?

00:26:21.289 --> 00:26:27.750
And, partly, that is because you get 1,000
true fans by accumulating them one by one,

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:33.750
and if you are focused on today I'm going
to get one more additional customer, that

00:26:33.750 --> 00:26:37.070
is tremendously powerful.

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:42.900
Customer by customer, are they happy, am I
giving value to them?

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:47.190
If you can focus on that, that is incredibly
a superpower.

00:26:47.190 --> 00:26:52.049
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, for sure, and if you can
take those 1,000 true fans and some subset

00:26:52.049 --> 00:26:58.770
of them become your PR slash marketing forces,
then things can multiply very quickly.

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:01.430
I promised left turns, we're going to take
another left turn.

00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:04.510
Your failed campaign to discover all the species
of life on Earth.

00:27:04.510 --> 00:27:10.070
So we went, I wanted to hit a highlight, and
maybe this is also a highlight, but I would

00:27:10.070 --> 00:27:13.980
love for you to expand on this.

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:14.980
Lessons learned.

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:15.980
What happened?

00:27:15.980 --> 00:27:23.700
Kevin Kelly: So there was conversation I was
part of, and I was sitting next to a billionaire

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:32.080
who said, "It's actually hard to give away
a billion dollars," and for some reason I

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:39.130
thought at that moment, well, actually I know
what I would do, and that would be I would

00:27:39.130 --> 00:27:46.649
hire all the local indigenous people and have
them be barefoot taxonomists and go out and

00:27:46.649 --> 00:27:55.000
discover, and catalog count all the living
species on this planet because we've never

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:59.080
done that, and by the way, if we found life
on another planet, that's the first thing

00:27:59.080 --> 00:28:03.799
we would do is a systematic survey of all
the life on that planet, but we haven't done

00:28:03.799 --> 00:28:09.659
that on our own home planet, and because you're
paying locals, it would distribute that money

00:28:09.659 --> 00:28:12.039
down really, really fast.

00:28:12.039 --> 00:28:16.800
Stewart Brand was sitting next to me and he
thought it was a cool idea, and then I didn't

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:21.669
think anything more of it because I have ideas,
I have a lot of ideas, I'm giving it away,

00:28:21.669 --> 00:28:28.490
and then a week later, Stewart said, "Let's
do that idea," and I said, "What idea?"

00:28:28.490 --> 00:28:29.909
I'd forgotten about it already.

00:28:29.909 --> 00:28:38.679
He said, "You know, the idea at dinner about
accounting all the species," and I said, "Yeah,

00:28:38.679 --> 00:28:39.679
I don't know.

00:28:39.679 --> 00:28:40.679
It's like we're not taxonomists.

00:28:40.679 --> 00:28:48.480
I'm not a biologist," and Stewart's hunch
was that, with new technology, and this is

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:53.029
my bias too, we might be able to do that in
addition to it.

00:28:53.029 --> 00:29:00.039
Tim Ferriss: Could you just briefly explain
for people who don't know who Stewart Brand

00:29:00.039 --> 00:29:01.039
is?

00:29:01.039 --> 00:29:04.769
Kevin Kelly: So Stewart Brand is a close friend
and the person who first hired me.

00:29:04.769 --> 00:29:12.539
He invented The Whole Earth Catalog in 1969,
and the best way to describe The Whole Earth

00:29:12.539 --> 00:29:20.080
Catalog is it was kind of like your information
guide to the world before there was the internet.

00:29:20.080 --> 00:29:23.399
Steve Jobs famously called it "the internet
before there was an internet."

00:29:23.399 --> 00:29:28.250
It was internet printed on newsprint because
it was reader generated.

00:29:28.250 --> 00:29:33.779
So before YouTube, before anything, if you
wanted to find out how to build a house, or

00:29:33.779 --> 00:29:40.429
repair your VW bug, or start a homeschool,
or keep bees, where would you go?

00:29:40.429 --> 00:29:42.800
There was literally no place to find that
information.

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:44.480
Libraries didn't have that information.

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:49.539
There was no internet to look it up, but The
Whole Earth Catalog started to accumulate

00:29:49.539 --> 00:29:54.220
those, and there was, readers of it would
send in their versions like, oh, the best

00:29:54.220 --> 00:29:59.309
book on gold panning is this thing, and then
Stewart would run it, print it, and run it,

00:29:59.309 --> 00:30:00.549
right away.

00:30:00.549 --> 00:30:01.549
And there was no advertising.

00:30:01.549 --> 00:30:03.950
It was kind of reader supported.

00:30:03.950 --> 00:30:05.799
So that was Stewart Brand.

00:30:05.799 --> 00:30:08.850
He went on to do things.

00:30:08.850 --> 00:30:14.399
We started The Well together, which was the
first online access to the internet and other

00:30:14.399 --> 00:30:15.399
things.

00:30:15.399 --> 00:30:23.169
So, he's my hero, and he just had a recent
book, a biography written about him by John

00:30:23.169 --> 00:30:26.279
Markoff, The New York Times' tech writer,
and Markoff's theory is — and so anyway,

00:30:26.279 --> 00:30:32.470
Stewart has been sort of at the center or
at the leading edge of almost, first the beatniks,

00:30:32.470 --> 00:30:35.220
and then the hippies, and then the digital
thing —

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:38.220
Tim Ferriss: He's like the Forrest Gump of
25 seminal moments in history.

00:30:38.220 --> 00:30:39.220
Kevin Kelly: Right.

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:40.760
He's kind of always there.

00:30:40.760 --> 00:30:42.770
His background was biology.

00:30:42.770 --> 00:30:47.900
He was a biologist for a study with Paul Ehrlich,
who was The Population Bomb guy on the other

00:30:47.900 --> 00:30:50.169
side of this argument about population.

00:30:50.169 --> 00:30:57.429
So, Stewart was sitting there, Stewart started
along now with me, and Danny Hillis, and Peter

00:30:57.429 --> 00:31:02.980
Schwartz to think about, to encourage long-term
thinking, to be a good ancestor.

00:31:02.980 --> 00:31:04.320
How do we be good ancestors?

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:10.409
And, at that dinner, Stewart said, later on,
he said, we should really try and do this.

00:31:10.409 --> 00:31:12.450
It would be kind of a great thing.

00:31:12.450 --> 00:31:16.460
So, we actually started a foundation called
All Species.

00:31:16.460 --> 00:31:23.970
I named it All Species Inventory, All Species
Foundation, and we were going to try and raise

00:31:23.970 --> 00:31:28.120
money, not from the usual sources that funded
taxonomy.

00:31:28.120 --> 00:31:30.250
We didn't want to take any money from there
because it was really pitiful, the amount

00:31:30.250 --> 00:31:36.440
of money, but to find it from the Silicon
Valley and get money for developing the technology

00:31:36.440 --> 00:31:41.059
that would be able to do that, and it was
just too early.

00:31:41.059 --> 00:31:43.149
It was just too early.

00:31:43.149 --> 00:31:45.140
Tim Ferriss: Too early in what respects?

00:31:45.140 --> 00:31:52.100
Kevin Kelly: Well, right now, on my phone,
I have Merlin, which will identify a bird

00:31:52.100 --> 00:31:53.840
song.

00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:58.210
I have [Seek], which will identify almost
any plant or mushroom.

00:31:58.210 --> 00:31:59.210
That's what we needed.

00:31:59.210 --> 00:32:00.450
Tim Ferriss: I see, technologically speaking.

00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:05.809
Kevin Kelly: Yes, and you needed it because
even those aren't going to identify a new

00:32:05.809 --> 00:32:11.779
one, if you have an app that can identify
the known ones, then you're only going to

00:32:11.779 --> 00:32:16.630
bother the taxonomists with one that isn't
identified, so right now, otherwise, people

00:32:16.630 --> 00:32:17.721
are just sending the about, oh, this is a
—

00:32:17.721 --> 00:32:18.721
Tim Ferriss: Too much replication.

00:32:18.721 --> 00:32:22.200
Kevin Kelly: — brand new species and they're
saying, no, this is not a new one, don't bother

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:23.200
me with that one.

00:32:23.200 --> 00:32:29.600
So that's what we needed, and we were just
25 years too early in terms of technology

00:32:29.600 --> 00:32:35.389
being available to be able to assist this,
and so it became kind of a catalog of existing

00:32:35.389 --> 00:32:42.150
species, and that was the thing that shocked
us, was, okay, well first we need is a list

00:32:42.150 --> 00:32:44.660
of all the existing species.

00:32:44.660 --> 00:32:47.890
This is whatever this is, 2008 or something.

00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:48.890
There isn't one.

00:32:48.890 --> 00:32:51.050
It was like, what?

00:32:51.050 --> 00:32:57.840
Well, there's all these taxonomic publications
that they're all buried in these obscure publications

00:32:57.840 --> 00:32:59.410
that haven't been digitized yet.

00:32:59.410 --> 00:33:03.481
It was like, so I was like, oh, my gosh, this
is even further behind than we thought, so

00:33:03.481 --> 00:33:05.710
that is sort of what it became.

00:33:05.710 --> 00:33:14.200
It became kind of a program just to digitize
the existing known species, and then the other

00:33:14.200 --> 00:33:18.470
thing is that, as they started to do that,
they realized that there was this huge duplication

00:33:18.470 --> 00:33:22.950
of species having more than one name because
there being, you know, somebody in Germany

00:33:22.950 --> 00:33:27.850
and somebody in Japan, and not even knowing
that they're talking about the same thing.

00:33:27.850 --> 00:33:33.279
So it was failed in the sense that we still
don't know all the species on this planet.

00:33:33.279 --> 00:33:41.559
We don't even know how many we don't know,
and we're still only beginning to have a central,

00:33:41.559 --> 00:33:49.382
integrated, comprehensive, complete catalog
of what we do know, and it's called the Encyclopedia

00:33:49.382 --> 00:33:50.382
of Life, and E.O.

00:33:50.382 --> 00:33:54.059
Wilson, before he died, was involved in that,
Tim Ferriss: The legendary E.O.

00:33:54.059 --> 00:33:55.059
Wilson.

00:33:55.059 --> 00:33:56.059
Kevin Kelly: Right.

00:33:56.059 --> 00:33:57.059
Tim Ferriss: Mr. Consilience.

00:33:57.059 --> 00:33:58.059
Kevin Kelly: Yes.

00:33:58.059 --> 00:33:59.059
Tim Ferriss: Stewart Brand.

00:33:59.059 --> 00:34:01.450
Let's spend a little more time on Stewart
Brand.

00:34:01.450 --> 00:34:03.159
Kevin Kelly: Who has been a guest on your
show.

00:34:03.159 --> 00:34:04.550
Tim Ferriss: He has been a guest.

00:34:04.550 --> 00:34:05.720
He's spectacular.

00:34:05.720 --> 00:34:08.929
He is, what would you say his age is now?

00:34:08.929 --> 00:34:12.480
Kevin Kelly: He's 86, maybe.

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:19.470
Tim Ferriss: Something like that, and I interviewed
him not that long ago, and he was doing CrossFit

00:34:19.470 --> 00:34:21.000
two or three times a week.

00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:22.000
Kevin Kelly: Yes, right.

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:23.000
Exactly.

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:27.430
Tim Ferriss: Also, military background, you
just have to read his bio to even begin to

00:34:27.430 --> 00:34:29.089
try to believe it.

00:34:29.089 --> 00:34:34.720
He would be, also, maybe on your short list
for real-world most interesting men in the

00:34:34.720 --> 00:34:35.720
world.

00:34:35.720 --> 00:34:36.720
Kevin Kelly: Yes, exactly.

00:34:36.720 --> 00:34:45.139
Tim Ferriss: Now, I recall chatting with Stewart,
I believe, about resurrection of species.

00:34:45.139 --> 00:34:54.310
So the potential of Jurassic Park style, resurrecting,
say, woolly mammoths and reintroducing some

00:34:54.310 --> 00:35:00.520
of these large, terrestrial, herbivores for
any host of reasons.

00:35:00.520 --> 00:35:04.770
What do you think the future holds for those
types of plans?

00:35:04.770 --> 00:35:09.670
Kevin Kelly: Yeah, so Stewart, Ryan Phelan,
and I, who did All Species, and Stewart and

00:35:09.670 --> 00:35:14.839
Ryan went on to do the Revive and Restore
is the name of their program, and it is to

00:35:14.839 --> 00:35:21.990
originally, the totem animal was the mammoth,
the woolly mammoth, was to bring that back,

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:26.500
and there are a lot of very interesting reasons
why to do that, and the way that they do,

00:35:26.500 --> 00:35:33.060
is basically to take existing Asian elephants
and winterizing them through breeding.

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:34.450
Tim Ferriss: I got it.

00:35:34.450 --> 00:35:35.560
Kevin Kelly: Accelerated breeding.

00:35:35.560 --> 00:35:40.990
So you're not just going to hatch out of a
test tube, a brand new woolly mammoth.

00:35:40.990 --> 00:35:45.970
There would be a sense in which you would
kind of reuse the line of existing elephants

00:35:45.970 --> 00:35:48.220
to try and reverse engineer that.

00:35:48.220 --> 00:35:50.510
Tim Ferriss: You take a Mendelian approach
to —

00:35:50.510 --> 00:35:55.770
Kevin Kelly: But that's a little bit longer
term, and actually, Stewart and I went to,

00:35:55.770 --> 00:36:03.880
and George Church, went to Siberia to go get
samples of the mammoths that were being exposed

00:36:03.880 --> 00:36:04.890
by the thawing permafrost.

00:36:04.890 --> 00:36:05.890
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:36:05.890 --> 00:36:09.020
Kevin Kelly: So to get the DNA from the right,
through the trunk —

00:36:09.020 --> 00:36:10.569
Tim Ferriss: Jurassic Park Seven, scene one.

00:36:10.569 --> 00:36:11.569
Kevin Kelly: Right, right.

00:36:11.569 --> 00:36:12.569
Tim Ferriss: Opening.

00:36:12.569 --> 00:36:18.410
Kevin Kelly: Exactly, so that was quite an
experience, but there are other animals that

00:36:18.410 --> 00:36:22.590
are closer to being able to, they're going
to be a little bit easier to do it.

00:36:22.590 --> 00:36:24.400
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:36:24.400 --> 00:36:26.800
All right, well, optimism.

00:36:26.800 --> 00:36:33.920
You're one of the most optimistic people I
know, I think, that is a great influence on

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:38.859
me and the world, and I sometimes push back
on some of it.

00:36:38.859 --> 00:36:40.950
Kevin Kelly: Of course.

00:36:40.950 --> 00:36:42.000
You should.

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:47.460
Tim Ferriss: So what would be your, doesn't
have to be an elevator pitch, could just be

00:36:47.460 --> 00:36:50.321
a very long elevator ride because we have
infinite time —

00:36:50.321 --> 00:36:51.460
Kevin Kelly: 30 floors.

00:36:51.460 --> 00:36:52.600
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, exactly.

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:53.600
That's right.

00:36:53.600 --> 00:36:58.720
Moving as slowly as you would like — what
is the argument for why we should be optimistic?

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:03.290
Kevin Kelly: Generally, people see optimism
as kind of a temperament, it's a sunny view,

00:37:03.290 --> 00:37:07.430
and I think there is some of that, and I have
a natural amount of it, but over time, I've

00:37:07.430 --> 00:37:10.790
actually become even more optimistic than
my general tendency, deliberately.

00:37:10.790 --> 00:37:16.050
It's kind of like a learned optimism, and
I think the reason we should be as optimistic

00:37:16.050 --> 00:37:27.310
as we can is because it is how we make really
good things, good, complicated things.

00:37:27.310 --> 00:37:28.310
It's hard enough.

00:37:28.310 --> 00:37:31.960
Well, I mean, it's very hard to make good
complicated things work because generally

00:37:31.960 --> 00:37:38.440
there's more way things can fail than they
can succeed, and it's very unlikely that we're

00:37:38.440 --> 00:37:44.840
going to make something really good that's
complicated, inadvertently.

00:37:44.840 --> 00:37:46.320
They're hard to do.

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:53.450
So we have to see it and believe that it can
be done, and that is where the optimism comes

00:37:53.450 --> 00:37:59.290
in, is envisioning something and then believing
that you could make it real.

00:37:59.290 --> 00:38:05.140
Because, when we look back on history, and
that's where a lot of my optimism comes, we

00:38:05.140 --> 00:38:11.210
realize that most of the things that we have
now have been made by people who are optimistic.

00:38:11.210 --> 00:38:15.230
Reviewing that it was possible to make them,
and believe that they were going to make them,

00:38:15.230 --> 00:38:16.470
and could imagine them.

00:38:16.470 --> 00:38:22.800
So, I think of it as a work of imagination
where you kind of imagine a good scenario,

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:27.400
which is harder to do than imagining a scenario
where it fails or collapses.

00:38:27.400 --> 00:38:33.800
It is much easier to imagine how things break
than it is to see how they work, and that's

00:38:33.800 --> 00:38:39.960
why entrepreneurs and all the others are rightly
lauded because they're going against that

00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:40.960
grain.

00:38:40.960 --> 00:38:46.450
It is hard to imagine how we could have this
thing that seems like it is improbable, and

00:38:46.450 --> 00:38:48.910
most things that work are improbable.

00:38:48.910 --> 00:38:54.340
That's the definition from the Santa Fe complexity
theory, is that things breaking down is the

00:38:54.340 --> 00:38:56.800
probable.

00:38:56.800 --> 00:38:59.500
Complicated things working are improbable
by definition.

00:38:59.500 --> 00:39:01.740
And, so you're against the improbable.

00:39:01.740 --> 00:39:09.950
And that work of imagining the improbable
and having the improbable succeed, and believing

00:39:09.950 --> 00:39:15.349
it can, is optimism, which means that the
optimists are the ones who shape our future.

00:39:15.349 --> 00:39:22.780
So I'd like to give a little story of a car,
and you need to have brakes on the car to

00:39:22.780 --> 00:39:26.950
steer the car —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I'm with you so far.

00:39:26.950 --> 00:39:31.720
Kevin Kelly: — but the engine is actually
the more important element, and so there are

00:39:31.720 --> 00:39:36.250
people and there are organizations, and there
are methods that are going to be doing the

00:39:36.250 --> 00:39:39.760
braking, and I think they're essential.

00:39:39.760 --> 00:39:46.720
I want brakes in the car, but I just feel
that the brake can overwhelm and cause stagnation,

00:39:46.720 --> 00:39:53.180
and that we also wanted to remember to focus
on making the engine even stronger, and so

00:39:53.180 --> 00:39:54.859
I emphasized the engine.

00:39:54.859 --> 00:39:59.050
Tim Ferriss: So I want to take a closer look
at the engine.

00:39:59.050 --> 00:40:05.650
So if things breaking down are the probable,
and there are many more ways things can go

00:40:05.650 --> 00:40:14.740
wrong than they can go, if I'm hearing you
correctly, and maybe also bringing in some

00:40:14.740 --> 00:40:20.890
of my own position, it would be that you can
have, that active optimism is probably more

00:40:20.890 --> 00:40:23.859
valuable than passive optimism in the sense
that —

00:40:23.859 --> 00:40:24.859
Kevin Kelly: Yes, yes.

00:40:24.859 --> 00:40:29.090
Tim Ferriss: — the belief that you can make
things turn out all right, as opposed to the

00:40:29.090 --> 00:40:38.550
belief that things will turn out all right,
and therefore, I can go about my day and not

00:40:38.550 --> 00:40:44.619
concern myself with worries about A, B, C,
D, E, all the way through Z, and I'm curious

00:40:44.619 --> 00:40:54.500
if you suddenly had The Kevin Kelly Institute
for Active Optimists, how you would cultivate

00:40:54.500 --> 00:41:06.270
this, or maybe encourage it in more people
because I do see optimists who are, not panicked,

00:41:06.270 --> 00:41:13.970
not necessarily paranoid, but they are very
interested and excited and feel some moral

00:41:13.970 --> 00:41:17.660
obligation to focus on really high leverage,
solving really high leverage problems, or

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:20.040
creating new technologies.

00:41:20.040 --> 00:41:26.480
I also see techno optimists that are like,
well, if A, B, and C gets bad enough, if the

00:41:26.480 --> 00:41:32.740
temperature of the Earth gets to X, Y, and
Z, then we'll have these technologies, it

00:41:32.740 --> 00:41:38.069
will all be fine, and if this happens, then
that'll all be fine, and people thought oil

00:41:38.069 --> 00:41:43.390
was going to run out by this year, but they
didn't factor in that as the number of barrels

00:41:43.390 --> 00:41:47.650
per year produced went down, the price would
go up, and then all these other technologies,

00:41:47.650 --> 00:41:50.710
like fracking, became viable, and voila, no
problem.

00:41:50.710 --> 00:41:54.730
I view those caps as somewhat different, and
I'm just wondering if you have any perspectives

00:41:54.730 --> 00:41:55.730
on that.

00:41:55.730 --> 00:41:58.780
Kevin Kelly: I love your distinction between
passive optimism and active.

00:41:58.780 --> 00:42:04.640
I think that's brilliant and right on, but
I guess the reality is that you can't be active

00:42:04.640 --> 00:42:05.640
about everything.

00:42:05.640 --> 00:42:07.310
You have to select —
Tim Ferriss: Totally.

00:42:07.310 --> 00:42:11.760
Kevin Kelly: — and choose, and so there
is a sense in which, okay, there is a greater

00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:19.260
than zero chance that the Earth could be impacted
by an asteroid, and it would be really devastating,

00:42:19.260 --> 00:42:23.220
and one of the most devastating things that
could ever happen to this planet, far beyond,

00:42:23.220 --> 00:42:24.960
even with climate change.

00:42:24.960 --> 00:42:26.869
And —
Tim Ferriss: It would definitely change the

00:42:26.869 --> 00:42:27.869
climate.

00:42:27.869 --> 00:42:32.619
Kevin Kelly: Exactly, and very fast, so it's
really good that there is now a group of people

00:42:32.619 --> 00:42:39.750
who are thinking about that, and there's the
B1612 Foundation, which is just tracking all

00:42:39.750 --> 00:42:41.690
the asteroids, what was it called?

00:42:41.690 --> 00:42:43.559
B1612, after The Little Prince.

00:42:43.559 --> 00:42:45.609
Tim Ferriss: Okay, got it.

00:42:45.609 --> 00:42:50.869
Kevin Kelly: It's a viable thing that they've
been behind all the tracking of all the asteroids

00:42:50.869 --> 00:42:59.079
and upping that, and then, recently, we just
sent something that hit the asteroid and deflected,

00:42:59.079 --> 00:43:03.589
so that's the first cosmic impact we really
had in the cosmos.

00:43:03.589 --> 00:43:06.930
Tim Ferriss: Pretty wild to think, like the
monkeys on a spinning rock —

00:43:06.930 --> 00:43:07.930
Kevin Kelly: Exactly.

00:43:07.930 --> 00:43:08.930
Right.

00:43:08.930 --> 00:43:09.930
Tim Ferriss: — figured out to deflect asteroids.

00:43:09.930 --> 00:43:12.920
Kevin Kelly: So it's good that there is a
small group of people, but we don't need to

00:43:12.920 --> 00:43:16.260
have that be our concern for making national
policy every year.

00:43:16.260 --> 00:43:23.569
Really, that probability is so low, that it
shouldn't really be a factor in us making

00:43:23.569 --> 00:43:25.550
our decisions about what we're going to do
this year.

00:43:25.550 --> 00:43:29.940
So that's passive, in that sense, but it is,
I can be passive about it because there is

00:43:29.940 --> 00:43:31.359
another group of people that is active about
it.

00:43:31.359 --> 00:43:32.810
Tim Ferriss: And you know that a group is
active.

00:43:32.810 --> 00:43:34.050
Kevin Kelly: Right, so what might help other
people?

00:43:34.050 --> 00:43:39.240
I think for me, one of the major things for
me was the more I thought about the future,

00:43:39.240 --> 00:43:43.910
the more I became interested in history, and
the more I read history, the more reality

00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:46.980
of progress became.

00:43:46.980 --> 00:43:54.600
I think just acknowledging the reality of
progress would go a large, huge step in helping

00:43:54.600 --> 00:43:55.600
our optimism.

00:43:55.600 --> 00:43:59.079
Tim Ferriss: Now, I'm interrupting, not to
push back, but just for definition of terms.

00:43:59.079 --> 00:44:02.849
What do you mean by "progress," because that
word can be used to mean in a lot of different

00:44:02.849 --> 00:44:03.849
things.

00:44:03.849 --> 00:44:04.849
In what sense?

00:44:04.849 --> 00:44:05.849
Kevin Kelly: That's right.

00:44:05.849 --> 00:44:09.001
It's a very loaded word, and I'm using it
to mean, simply, that —

00:44:09.001 --> 00:44:12.490
Tim Ferriss: Angels of our better nature type
stuff?

00:44:12.490 --> 00:44:13.600
Kevin Kelly: Yes.

00:44:13.600 --> 00:44:15.089
How would I say it?

00:44:15.089 --> 00:44:24.060
It means that, overall, on average, this is
a better place to live than at any time in

00:44:24.060 --> 00:44:26.050
the past, and this is the kind of Obama test.

00:44:26.050 --> 00:44:30.520
I don't know if you've heard about that, but
it's like if you were to be born randomly

00:44:30.520 --> 00:44:38.500
in any time period, it could be male, female,
poor, rich, you're totally at random, on some

00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:40.329
average thing.

00:44:40.329 --> 00:44:41.410
What time do you want to live in?

00:44:41.410 --> 00:44:42.410
What time period?

00:44:42.410 --> 00:44:47.930
And there's no way you want to be anything
before, at least, 50 years ago, and maybe

00:44:47.930 --> 00:44:51.030
not even within 50 years.

00:44:51.030 --> 00:44:56.700
So, because we intuitively understand that
this is actually the best time to be alive,

00:44:56.700 --> 00:45:01.290
but there is a recognition of, so what are
the currents that made that?

00:45:01.290 --> 00:45:02.290
What has allowed that?

00:45:02.290 --> 00:45:03.290
What's operating?

00:45:03.290 --> 00:45:05.080
Is it still going?

00:45:05.080 --> 00:45:11.490
And from my view of history, and I had the
chance to live in the past, and on a time

00:45:11.490 --> 00:45:12.920
machine, we'll talk about that in a minute
—

00:45:12.920 --> 00:45:13.920
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:45:13.920 --> 00:45:15.829
Wow, I didn't see that coming.

00:45:15.829 --> 00:45:16.829
Yes, I got time.

00:45:16.829 --> 00:45:17.829
I'm taking note.

00:45:17.829 --> 00:45:18.829
Time machine, question mark.

00:45:18.829 --> 00:45:25.240
Kevin Kelly: I've been in the time machine
and it's very, very clear that we've been

00:45:25.240 --> 00:45:34.010
on a momentum in a trajectory of progress,
and it's possible that could stop tomorrow.

00:45:34.010 --> 00:45:39.080
It's highly unlikely that it's going to stop
tomorrow, so there is just, all the conditions

00:45:39.080 --> 00:45:46.150
that make that, suggest that it will continue,
and so part of our optimism can come from

00:45:46.150 --> 00:45:47.150
that.

00:45:47.150 --> 00:45:48.330
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:45:48.330 --> 00:45:49.330
Time machine's coming up.

00:45:49.330 --> 00:45:50.330
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

00:45:50.330 --> 00:45:51.420
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I want people to stick around.

00:45:51.420 --> 00:45:53.050
Right after this commercial break, folks.

00:45:53.050 --> 00:46:00.490
No, I'm not going to take a commercial break,
but I do want to ask you, given the bet on

00:46:00.490 --> 00:46:10.319
collapse of global human population, do you
think that by 2060, if we've peaked out at

00:46:10.319 --> 00:46:16.770
the top of the roller coaster, do you think
this progress is inextricably linked to population

00:46:16.770 --> 00:46:23.660
growth and population density, and if that's
the case, do you think we might be looking

00:46:23.660 --> 00:46:24.660
at regression?

00:46:24.660 --> 00:46:25.660
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

00:46:25.660 --> 00:46:26.660
Yeah.

00:46:26.660 --> 00:46:27.700
Tim Ferriss: I'm not trying to turn you to
the dark side.

00:46:27.700 --> 00:46:28.700
Kevin Kelly: No, no, no, no.

00:46:28.700 --> 00:46:29.700
Tim Ferriss: I'm just curious.

00:46:29.700 --> 00:46:31.180
Kevin Kelly: You're doing, you're asking the
exact right question.

00:46:31.180 --> 00:46:35.380
I think there's a movement called Degrowth,
the degrowth movement.

00:46:35.380 --> 00:46:37.339
Tim Ferriss: I'm not familiar.

00:46:37.339 --> 00:46:44.640
Kevin Kelly: So these are people who basically
see the troubles of the world, in particular

00:46:44.640 --> 00:46:48.230
the climate one, coming from our addiction
to growth.

00:46:48.230 --> 00:46:52.960
That growth, this is kind of the consumer,
capitalistic kind of idea that grow, grow,

00:46:52.960 --> 00:46:55.819
grow, grow, and they're saying, it's finite.

00:46:55.819 --> 00:47:01.690
We can't continue to grow, and we have to
degrowth, stop growing, and there's a little

00:47:01.690 --> 00:47:06.829
bit of a confusion in English because there's
two meanings of the word growth.

00:47:06.829 --> 00:47:12.750
There's growth to add more pounds, to add
more and more stuff, to get bigger, wider,

00:47:12.750 --> 00:47:17.490
heavier, to have more things, to sell more
refrigerators, to sell more bottles of wine,

00:47:17.490 --> 00:47:21.940
but there's another meaning of the word growth,
which is probably closer to what you're interested

00:47:21.940 --> 00:47:22.940
in.

00:47:22.940 --> 00:47:26.609
Personal growth, developing, maturing —
Tim Ferriss: Or knowledge growth.

00:47:26.609 --> 00:47:27.609
Kevin Kelly: Knowledge growth.

00:47:27.609 --> 00:47:29.970
Tim Ferriss: I guess, in a sense, it's the
same as the first, but it's —

00:47:29.970 --> 00:47:31.300
Kevin Kelly: No, it's increasing its complexity.

00:47:31.300 --> 00:47:32.300
Tim Ferriss: Infinite in capacity.

00:47:32.300 --> 00:47:36.300
Kevin Kelly: It's taking the same number of
atoms and having a more complex arrangement.

00:47:36.300 --> 00:47:43.810
It's going from a jellyfish to a chimpanzee
or something, and so that complex, adaptive

00:47:43.810 --> 00:47:50.809
system, where you have increasing levels of
complexity and more exotropy in it, that is

00:47:50.809 --> 00:47:51.809
a different kind of growth.

00:47:51.809 --> 00:47:52.960
Tim Ferriss: What is exotropy?

00:47:52.960 --> 00:47:54.349
Now, I know what entropy is.

00:47:54.349 --> 00:47:55.349
Kevin Kelly: It's the opposite.

00:47:55.349 --> 00:47:58.380
Tim Ferriss: Exo, like exoskeleton —
Kevin Kelly: Right, so it's the definition

00:47:58.380 --> 00:48:05.640
of entropy, right, is increasing disorder,
and then there's something called negative

00:48:05.640 --> 00:48:08.890
entropy, which is what I'm talking about.

00:48:08.890 --> 00:48:10.350
But that's a double negative.

00:48:10.350 --> 00:48:11.500
I don't like double negatives.

00:48:11.500 --> 00:48:19.500
So exotropy is basically an increase in order.

00:48:19.500 --> 00:48:29.740
So exotropy is this idea of this increasing
order that comes at the cost of increasing

00:48:29.740 --> 00:48:30.740
entropy.

00:48:30.740 --> 00:48:31.740
So that's the thing.

00:48:31.740 --> 00:48:39.200
So you get your system like a living cell
is actually increasing the generation of entropy

00:48:39.200 --> 00:48:40.510
as it increases its order.

00:48:40.510 --> 00:48:42.559
It's like the magnifying glass with the sun.

00:48:42.559 --> 00:48:43.559
You ever seen that?

00:48:43.559 --> 00:48:44.559
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:48:44.559 --> 00:48:48.869
Kevin Kelly: There's a little bright spot
in the middle of a lens in the sun, but all

00:48:48.869 --> 00:48:50.691
around it is a shadow.

00:48:50.691 --> 00:48:52.501
Because it's taken all the light —
Tim Ferriss: Right, and concentrated it.

00:48:52.501 --> 00:48:53.501
Kevin Kelly: From around and concentrated
in.

00:48:53.501 --> 00:48:54.650
So there it generates a shadow.

00:48:54.650 --> 00:48:56.940
Tim Ferriss: Right, so they go together.

00:48:56.940 --> 00:48:57.940
Kevin Kelly: So they go together.

00:48:57.940 --> 00:49:00.460
So what type of growth is an increase in complexity?

00:49:00.460 --> 00:49:04.900
So you have an economy where instead of trying
to sell more bottles of wine, you try to sell

00:49:04.900 --> 00:49:07.339
the same number of wines, but better wine.

00:49:07.339 --> 00:49:08.740
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:49:08.740 --> 00:49:12.220
Kevin Kelly: That's a different kind of growth.

00:49:12.220 --> 00:49:15.810
That's the kind of growth that we can shift
into.

00:49:15.810 --> 00:49:18.839
So we're just increasing the quality of things.

00:49:18.839 --> 00:49:21.690
Tim Ferriss: Do you think there are incentives
that will drive that?

00:49:21.690 --> 00:49:24.920
Kevin Kelly: Well, the decreasing population.

00:49:24.920 --> 00:49:29.060
Tim Ferriss: How are we going to keep our
revenue numbers the same?

00:49:29.060 --> 00:49:30.060
Well, everybody's leaving.

00:49:30.060 --> 00:49:31.060
Kevin Kelly: Right.

00:49:31.060 --> 00:49:34.570
You have a smaller market every year, a smaller
audience.

00:49:34.570 --> 00:49:36.839
So one way is to make things better.

00:49:36.839 --> 00:49:37.990
To make better stuff.

00:49:37.990 --> 00:49:38.990
Okay.

00:49:38.990 --> 00:49:42.940
And we have, so refrigerators, if you just
count how many refrigerators are being sold,

00:49:42.940 --> 00:49:46.619
you can have increasing numbers, but you could
sell refrigerators and make them better every

00:49:46.619 --> 00:49:48.339
year, which is what we've been doing.

00:49:48.339 --> 00:49:50.790
And that's actually not often caught into
the accounted for in economics.

00:49:50.790 --> 00:49:57.640
It's just like it's GDP is how many refrigerators
per unit are you making, but they're not saying,

00:49:57.640 --> 00:50:00.500
"Well, actually these new refrigerators are
better because they use less energy.

00:50:00.500 --> 00:50:05.250
They make ice as well as refrigerate, they
do all these other things."

00:50:05.250 --> 00:50:07.760
That's not really accounted for.

00:50:07.760 --> 00:50:12.140
And so we can change how we account for things
and we start to measure something different

00:50:12.140 --> 00:50:16.960
just other than expansion, the more stuff.

00:50:16.960 --> 00:50:18.920
So we need some new metrics.

00:50:18.920 --> 00:50:28.579
So yes, I am optimistic that we can change
our understanding and what we aim for.

00:50:28.579 --> 00:50:30.470
It's not inevitable though.

00:50:30.470 --> 00:50:32.700
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:50:32.700 --> 00:50:36.809
Well, time will tell and makes me want to
be a better student of history.

00:50:36.809 --> 00:50:37.809
Also, as you pointed out.

00:50:37.809 --> 00:50:38.809
Kevin Kelly: So the time machine.

00:50:38.809 --> 00:50:40.050
I know that you're going to —
Tim Ferriss: Time machine.

00:50:40.050 --> 00:50:41.050
Kevin Kelly: I know you're going to ask about
the time machine.

00:50:41.050 --> 00:50:42.050
Tim Ferriss: Oh, it's there.

00:50:42.050 --> 00:50:43.270
It's right there at the top right.

00:50:43.270 --> 00:50:44.829
Time machine question mark.

00:50:44.829 --> 00:50:47.180
For those who think I'm lying.

00:50:47.180 --> 00:50:48.349
It's right there.

00:50:48.349 --> 00:50:49.349
Okay.

00:50:49.349 --> 00:50:50.349
Kevin Kelly: Time machine.

00:50:50.349 --> 00:50:58.030
I took a $20 bus ride in northern Afghanistan
in 1975 somewhere.

00:50:58.030 --> 00:51:06.390
And I arrived in a different century, literally
in a different century.

00:51:06.390 --> 00:51:07.390
I had no map.

00:51:07.390 --> 00:51:10.550
I had not met anybody, heard of anybody who
ever went there.

00:51:10.550 --> 00:51:14.700
I mean, there was obviously lots of Afghans,
but I mean no tourists.

00:51:14.700 --> 00:51:17.400
I had no idea if I could get there.

00:51:17.400 --> 00:51:20.670
It was literally a name on a very poor map.

00:51:20.670 --> 00:51:23.100
Tim Ferriss: How did you choose it?

00:51:23.100 --> 00:51:28.260
Kevin Kelly: It was so remote and I wanted
to see what was at the end of the line.

00:51:28.260 --> 00:51:29.260
Tim Ferriss: All right.

00:51:29.260 --> 00:51:31.880
Kevin Kelly: And here was a town, and I don't
know, maybe there's a hundred thousand people

00:51:31.880 --> 00:51:32.880
in this town.

00:51:32.880 --> 00:51:33.880
Tim Ferriss: That was a good size.

00:51:33.880 --> 00:51:34.880
Kevin Kelly: A good size town.

00:51:34.880 --> 00:51:39.521
There was no electricity in the town.

00:51:39.521 --> 00:51:41.090
They didn't have street lights.

00:51:41.090 --> 00:51:46.000
They would have a guy at night go and light
kerosene lamps, the street lighter.

00:51:46.000 --> 00:51:52.380
They would throw their shit into the strait,
I mean out the window kind of stuff.

00:51:52.380 --> 00:51:55.730
And of course, there was a futile structure.

00:51:55.730 --> 00:52:01.069
I mean, they had basically slaves and child
brides and the whole thing.

00:52:01.069 --> 00:52:04.890
It was just medieval in every way.

00:52:04.890 --> 00:52:05.890
Very little metal.

00:52:05.890 --> 00:52:07.920
There was no signage on the town.

00:52:07.920 --> 00:52:09.540
There was no signs.

00:52:09.540 --> 00:52:10.760
Didn't need signs.

00:52:10.760 --> 00:52:14.310
So it was like, I'm in a different century.

00:52:14.310 --> 00:52:16.839
I'm in a different century.

00:52:16.839 --> 00:52:24.900
And that experience of seeing what you get
when you had development and technology, and

00:52:24.900 --> 00:52:28.099
of course you could see all the challenges
and the problems.

00:52:28.099 --> 00:52:36.040
But the main thing that I learned from that
experience is the thing that we get is we

00:52:36.040 --> 00:52:38.059
get choices and options.

00:52:38.059 --> 00:52:40.550
That's ultimately what we get from the technology.

00:52:40.550 --> 00:52:44.930
So the people growing up there had — their
occupations were fated, they were destined.

00:52:44.930 --> 00:52:48.160
If you're going to to be male, you're going
to be a farmer, maybe a blacksmith.

00:52:48.160 --> 00:52:51.329
If you're a woman, you're going to be a wife
and a mother.

00:52:51.329 --> 00:52:52.440
And that was it.

00:52:52.440 --> 00:52:58.599
If you took the bus all the way into the city
and went somewhere else, you'd be in a grimy,

00:52:58.599 --> 00:53:01.109
gritty slum.

00:53:01.109 --> 00:53:05.000
But you had a choice for the first time of
what you could do.

00:53:05.000 --> 00:53:09.960
And maybe, not then, but now, if you took
that bus ride, you might be a web manager,

00:53:09.960 --> 00:53:13.760
web designer, a yoga teacher, a mortgage broker,
whatever.

00:53:13.760 --> 00:53:15.700
You have choices.

00:53:15.700 --> 00:53:17.400
And that's what they do not have.

00:53:17.400 --> 00:53:24.430
They had very strong family, good identity,
tremendous support, maybe organic food, but

00:53:24.430 --> 00:53:26.010
no choices.

00:53:26.010 --> 00:53:28.369
Tim Ferriss: AI.

00:53:28.369 --> 00:53:33.549
Kevin Kelly: From the 15th century to today.

00:53:33.549 --> 00:53:41.140
Tim Ferriss: Even as I understand it, some,
let's call them AI researchers, computer scientists

00:53:41.140 --> 00:53:49.310
with familiarity with AI, couldn't have even
predicted several years ago, us having today.

00:53:49.310 --> 00:53:53.650
Many choices, maybe some difficult choices,
maybe some difficult outcomes, I might go

00:53:53.650 --> 00:53:55.890
so far to say.

00:53:55.890 --> 00:54:00.100
And I wanted to read something.

00:54:00.100 --> 00:54:06.980
This is from your Wired piece, November, 2022.

00:54:06.980 --> 00:54:12.830
And this is after spending months creating
thousands of images using AI, excellent piece.

00:54:12.830 --> 00:54:17.190
I think it's limitless creativity.

00:54:17.190 --> 00:54:22.330
And there's one line that stuck out to me
and I was like man, that's a strong statement.

00:54:22.330 --> 00:54:26.660
I kind of wish Kevin hadn't included this
because I think it's going to be hard to defend

00:54:26.660 --> 00:54:28.900
and I would like to talk about it.

00:54:28.900 --> 00:54:35.910
And this also pairs with an article I only
started recently reading from Marc Andreessen.

00:54:35.910 --> 00:54:43.850
And as I understand it, the basic premise
is that AI will not cause an increase in unemployment.

00:54:43.850 --> 00:54:47.190
Which is a bit broader than the line that
we have here.

00:54:47.190 --> 00:54:48.430
So let me read it.

00:54:48.430 --> 00:54:52.671
"I have spent the past six months using AIs
to create thousands of striking images, often

00:54:52.671 --> 00:54:56.700
losing a night’s sleep in the unending quest
to find just one more beauty hidden in the

00:54:56.700 --> 00:54:57.700
code.

00:54:57.700 --> 00:55:01.470
And after interviewing the creators, power
users, and other early adopters of these generators,

00:55:01.470 --> 00:55:06.140
I can make a very clear prediction: Generative
AI will alter how we design just about everything."

00:55:06.140 --> 00:55:07.730
Period.

00:55:07.730 --> 00:55:09.760
Side note, I completely agree with this.

00:55:09.760 --> 00:55:13.760
"Oh, and not a single human artist will lose
their job because of this new technology."

00:55:13.760 --> 00:55:20.660
So maybe I should ask you to clarify this,
because I work with tons and tons and tons

00:55:20.660 --> 00:55:22.330
of contractors.

00:55:22.330 --> 00:55:29.049
And there are artists right now I've worked
with who are going to be replaced.

00:55:29.049 --> 00:55:32.650
At least some of their functions will be replaced
by AI.

00:55:32.650 --> 00:55:37.150
So I would predict they will lose that specific
job.

00:55:37.150 --> 00:55:40.670
Not necessarily with me, but at some point
in the next few years, they will probably

00:55:40.670 --> 00:55:41.920
lose that job.

00:55:41.920 --> 00:55:47.520
If they have adapted to using the technologies,
I think, or carved a niche for themselves,

00:55:47.520 --> 00:55:49.289
they will find another job.

00:55:49.289 --> 00:55:51.109
But they will lose jobs.

00:55:51.109 --> 00:55:52.109
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

00:55:52.109 --> 00:55:53.640
Tim Ferriss: So how would you expand on this
statement?

00:55:53.640 --> 00:55:58.539
Kevin Kelly: There might be a little bit of
semantics here because I would say that it

00:55:58.539 --> 00:56:00.770
will replace many tasks, but not their job.

00:56:00.770 --> 00:56:01.770
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:56:01.770 --> 00:56:04.289
Kevin Kelly: So this is what AI does, we replace
tasks.

00:56:04.289 --> 00:56:05.799
There are tasks that we do.

00:56:05.799 --> 00:56:08.240
Most jobs are complex with different tasks.

00:56:08.240 --> 00:56:11.809
And a lot of these tasks will go to the AI,
but not necessarily the job.

00:56:11.809 --> 00:56:12.809
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:56:12.809 --> 00:56:16.330
Kevin Kelly: Because the job will shift and
you'll have different tasks.

00:56:16.330 --> 00:56:21.130
Part of that strength is that I would actually
maybe even expand this even broader.

00:56:21.130 --> 00:56:27.430
And people, and I welcome feedback on this,
my claim would be that I don't think there's

00:56:27.430 --> 00:56:31.820
anybody in any field that's lost their job
because of AI.

00:56:31.820 --> 00:56:32.820
So far.

00:56:32.820 --> 00:56:37.580
There's tasks that have gone away, but not
jobs.

00:56:37.580 --> 00:56:42.710
And a lot of the worry about this AI is what
I call third-person worry.

00:56:42.710 --> 00:56:46.520
They're saying, "My job/hobby, I'm not going
to be replaced."

00:56:46.520 --> 00:56:52.039
But I can imagine somebody else, or maybe
I can imagine my friend losing it, but I'm

00:56:52.039 --> 00:56:55.380
still waiting for someone to say, "I lost
my job."

00:56:55.380 --> 00:57:01.050
A real person with a real name who'd lost
their job because of AI.

00:57:01.050 --> 00:57:03.680
And so far, I haven't.

00:57:03.680 --> 00:57:09.020
I maybe even offer like a $200 bounty if you
could tell me the name, specific person who

00:57:09.020 --> 00:57:12.260
lost their job because of AI of any sort.

00:57:12.260 --> 00:57:16.000
And it's because —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, why take such a binary

00:57:16.000 --> 00:57:18.000
bet?

00:57:18.000 --> 00:57:19.000
I know you like these bets.

00:57:19.000 --> 00:57:22.710
I would take the opposite side of that bet.

00:57:22.710 --> 00:57:23.710
But please continue.

00:57:23.710 --> 00:57:26.430
Kevin Kelly: Well, you can take it by giving
a name.

00:57:26.430 --> 00:57:27.970
So it's partly —
Tim Ferriss: I just have to fire somebody

00:57:27.970 --> 00:57:29.180
and then I can take the $200.

00:57:29.180 --> 00:57:30.349
Kevin Kelly: Well, no.

00:57:30.349 --> 00:57:31.810
But you're at the AI.

00:57:31.810 --> 00:57:33.140
That's what I'm saying, because of AI.

00:57:33.140 --> 00:57:35.260
Tim Ferriss: Well, I would've to replace him
with AI.

00:57:35.260 --> 00:57:36.549
Kevin Kelly: Yes.

00:57:36.549 --> 00:57:38.470
Tim Ferriss: And then I can blame it on AI.

00:57:38.470 --> 00:57:39.470
Kevin Kelly: Right.

00:57:39.470 --> 00:57:40.470
Okay.

00:57:40.470 --> 00:57:41.470
And we won't be able to do that right now.

00:57:41.470 --> 00:57:42.470
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:57:42.470 --> 00:57:43.470
Let's take an example, if I may.

00:57:43.470 --> 00:57:44.470
Kevin Kelly: Yes.

00:57:44.470 --> 00:57:45.470
Tim Ferriss: Logo design.

00:57:45.470 --> 00:57:46.470
Kevin Kelly: Yes.

00:57:46.470 --> 00:57:48.039
Tim Ferriss: That is what somebody does day
in, day out.

00:57:48.039 --> 00:57:49.039
Kevin Kelly: Right.

00:57:49.039 --> 00:57:50.039
Tim Ferriss: They design logos.

00:57:50.039 --> 00:57:51.039
Kevin Kelly: Right.

00:57:51.039 --> 00:57:53.190
And I've gone to some of the logo designers,
AI logo.

00:57:53.190 --> 00:57:57.470
There are logo AI designers right now and
they're amazing.

00:57:57.470 --> 00:58:01.819
But here's the position, and this is my position
is what we get from these AIs currently right

00:58:01.819 --> 00:58:05.450
now, are universal personal interns.

00:58:05.450 --> 00:58:07.599
They're intern, they're doing the work of
interns.

00:58:07.599 --> 00:58:08.849
Tim Ferriss: UPIs.

00:58:08.849 --> 00:58:10.099
Kevin Kelly: UPIs.

00:58:10.099 --> 00:58:11.099
Okay.

00:58:11.099 --> 00:58:15.010
And they're really amazing, but you have to
check their work.

00:58:15.010 --> 00:58:21.589
It's embarrassing to release their work without
improvement.

00:58:21.589 --> 00:58:23.010
The intern work.

00:58:23.010 --> 00:58:29.640
So I've used these logo AI generators and
I'll work with them over and over again.

00:58:29.640 --> 00:58:32.150
And this is what the artist will be doing.

00:58:32.150 --> 00:58:37.339
The artist is going to be working with their
interns, generating all these possibilities,

00:58:37.339 --> 00:58:38.349
tweaking them.

00:58:38.349 --> 00:58:41.770
They're kind of like a director or a conductor.

00:58:41.770 --> 00:58:50.790
They're managing the interns and they're not
releasing the intern work unedited, unpolished,

00:58:50.790 --> 00:58:52.609
uncurated.

00:58:52.609 --> 00:58:55.119
And that's what their new tasks become.

00:58:55.119 --> 00:58:56.579
The artists.

00:58:56.579 --> 00:59:00.949
Tim Ferriss: I'm not totally convinced, however,
I think that will happen.

00:59:00.949 --> 00:59:05.810
But I do think some rank and file will perhaps
need to find new jobs.

00:59:05.810 --> 00:59:13.030
At the very least, if someone has AI as the
UPI, I would imagine if you have a brand design

00:59:13.030 --> 00:59:19.141
studio that focuses on logos with 30 employees,
some of which are junior, there might be some

00:59:19.141 --> 00:59:20.141
shuffle.

00:59:20.141 --> 00:59:21.141
But we can — go ahead.

00:59:21.141 --> 00:59:27.109
Kevin Kelly: So, I mean there's going to be
a lot of art generated from these entities,

00:59:27.109 --> 00:59:31.390
these AIs, and I always want to say plural,
always plural.

00:59:31.390 --> 00:59:32.450
There's not one AI.

00:59:32.450 --> 00:59:35.319
There's AIs, all different species.

00:59:35.319 --> 00:59:41.580
But most of that work is being used for areas
that are blank now, where there is no pictures,

00:59:41.580 --> 00:59:43.349
where there isn't anything.

00:59:43.349 --> 00:59:49.380
So I have, my assistant actually has for years
woken up in the middle of the night to write

00:59:49.380 --> 00:59:50.799
her dreams down.

00:59:50.799 --> 00:59:55.801
And now she feeds those dreams into the AI
and she illustrates them.

00:59:55.801 --> 00:59:57.460
And they're just amazing.

00:59:57.460 --> 00:59:59.710
There were no illustrations before.

00:59:59.710 --> 01:00:00.710
Now they're illustrations.

01:00:00.710 --> 01:00:01.710
Tim Ferriss: Yup.

01:00:01.710 --> 01:00:05.200
Kevin Kelly: I used them to generate images
for my slides.

01:00:05.200 --> 01:00:07.350
There were no pictures before.

01:00:07.350 --> 01:00:08.350
Now there are pictures.

01:00:08.350 --> 01:00:10.710
So it's not like I'm replacing somebody —
Tim Ferriss: It's not a zero sum.

01:00:10.710 --> 01:00:11.710
Kevin Kelly: I'm filling it in.

01:00:11.710 --> 01:00:12.710
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:00:12.710 --> 01:00:18.730
Kevin Kelly: So the major, and by the way,
there's about 30 million brand new, never-seen-before

01:00:18.730 --> 01:00:26.020
images generated every day with these image
generators. 30 million.

01:00:26.020 --> 01:00:31.719
And I would say about maybe 95 percent or
maybe 98 percent of them, there's an audience

01:00:31.719 --> 01:00:34.069
of one.

01:00:34.069 --> 01:00:38.100
It's for the pure pleasure of seeing this.

01:00:38.100 --> 01:00:44.099
It's like you would take a walk out into nature
and just see a beautiful scene.

01:00:44.099 --> 01:00:46.030
It's like, "I'm just enjoying this."

01:00:46.030 --> 01:00:48.910
This is why they're mostly being generated.

01:00:48.910 --> 01:00:52.470
The predominant number of them, just because
they're beautiful.

01:00:52.470 --> 01:00:53.470
Okay?

01:00:53.470 --> 01:00:55.369
And so they weren't there before.

01:00:55.369 --> 01:00:59.940
You could not have your own private museum
of these really cool images, maybe no one

01:00:59.940 --> 01:01:01.140
will ever see again.

01:01:01.140 --> 01:01:02.140
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:01:02.140 --> 01:01:03.140
Kevin Kelly: Okay.

01:01:03.140 --> 01:01:07.960
And so that's what they're being mostly used
for is filling in the blank spaces.

01:01:07.960 --> 01:01:12.460
And that's also true, again, of a lot of the
other intern work that may be writing things

01:01:12.460 --> 01:01:15.869
that nobody else but the boss sees.

01:01:15.869 --> 01:01:18.099
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:01:18.099 --> 01:01:20.570
Let's look at this a little more closely.

01:01:20.570 --> 01:01:25.320
So I will say, just as a means of setting
the table, I'm deeply, deeply interested in

01:01:25.320 --> 01:01:31.830
these tools, which is why, and the effects
that they will have on the creative economies,

01:01:31.830 --> 01:01:36.160
the economy period, broadly speaking, society.

01:01:36.160 --> 01:01:38.020
I think they're very underestimated.

01:01:38.020 --> 01:01:39.440
Kevin Kelly: Yes.

01:01:39.440 --> 01:01:43.900
Tim Ferriss: And I'd love to get your take
on that in a bit.

01:01:43.900 --> 01:01:49.890
I've run AI art competitions related to some
of the fiction that I've put out and have

01:01:49.890 --> 01:01:52.109
been absolutely blown away.

01:01:52.109 --> 01:02:01.290
I also sympathize with some artists, say on
ArtStation or DeviantArt, who are part of

01:02:01.290 --> 01:02:06.029
the training set who are popularly mimicked,
right?

01:02:06.029 --> 01:02:08.750
Prompt, yada, yada, yada.

01:02:08.750 --> 01:02:11.900
In the style of, fill in the blank.

01:02:11.900 --> 01:02:17.480
And I can understand why these artists would
be upset, feel threatened, maybe be financially

01:02:17.480 --> 01:02:18.480
impacted.

01:02:18.480 --> 01:02:20.150
I imagine their commission work might be.

01:02:20.150 --> 01:02:22.369
How do you think that'll shake out?

01:02:22.369 --> 01:02:29.299
And I know based on some of the conversations
that we've had, that I believe your perspective

01:02:29.299 --> 01:02:34.460
is if people are relevant, they're going to
be copied anyway, right?

01:02:34.460 --> 01:02:35.460
Kevin Kelly: Mm-hmm.

01:02:35.460 --> 01:02:39.400
Tim Ferriss: And if they're not, it doesn't
really matter if they're in the training data.

01:02:39.400 --> 01:02:40.400
Something along those lines.

01:02:40.400 --> 01:02:45.029
And so Picasso's influence is going to be
seen all over the place no matter what.

01:02:45.029 --> 01:02:49.100
But how do you think this will shake out in
the next handful of years?

01:02:49.100 --> 01:02:52.660
Because I understand why people would have
an aversion as artists.

01:02:52.660 --> 01:02:53.660
Kevin Kelly: Sure, sure.

01:02:53.660 --> 01:03:00.010
So I think there will be people, companies
who will make training sets.

01:03:00.010 --> 01:03:07.839
They're all opt-in in some capacity, maybe
most of it's already out of copyright.

01:03:07.839 --> 01:03:13.371
And they'll be sold as greenwashes, ethical
training sets, whatever it is.

01:03:13.371 --> 01:03:14.700
Tim Ferriss: Fair trade AI artwork.

01:03:14.700 --> 01:03:16.069
Kevin Kelly: Exactly.

01:03:16.069 --> 01:03:19.490
And then there could be a lot of them where
people will use it.

01:03:19.490 --> 01:03:22.940
They'll train the things on their own work.

01:03:22.940 --> 01:03:26.200
Like, help me make more images in my style.

01:03:26.200 --> 01:03:28.710
Tim Ferriss: I am doing some experiments with
that right now.

01:03:28.710 --> 01:03:29.950
Kevin Kelly: Exactly.

01:03:29.950 --> 01:03:36.950
And then there's going to be this ability
over time to require less of a training set.

01:03:36.950 --> 01:03:41.420
For right now we can, only way we train these
is the more, the billions, the better.

01:03:41.420 --> 01:03:47.950
But a human toddler can learn the difference
between a cat and a dog just with 12 examples.

01:03:47.950 --> 01:03:53.119
And when we start to have more targeted like
that, I think people will start to clamor

01:03:53.119 --> 01:03:54.640
to be included in the training set.

01:03:54.640 --> 01:04:02.549
Tim Ferriss: What needs to happen for the
AIs to require far fewer examples in these

01:04:02.549 --> 01:04:03.920
training data sets?

01:04:03.920 --> 01:04:07.510
Kevin Kelly: We don't know.

01:04:07.510 --> 01:04:08.510
That's the short answer.

01:04:08.510 --> 01:04:09.510
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

01:04:09.510 --> 01:04:10.510
But we suspect it's inevitable.

01:04:10.510 --> 01:04:12.160
Kevin Kelly: Well, we have existence proof
because we have toddlers.

01:04:12.160 --> 01:04:13.160
Tim Ferriss: Toddlers.

01:04:13.160 --> 01:04:14.160
Kevin Kelly: So many.

01:04:14.160 --> 01:04:19.339
But it may require, right now there's kind
of a brute force, these neuralnets.

01:04:19.339 --> 01:04:24.680
Brute force meaning that they're very flat
and they didn't work in the beginning because

01:04:24.680 --> 01:04:26.160
they weren't big enough.

01:04:26.160 --> 01:04:31.730
And the bigger we make them, they seem to
overcome a lot of the problems.

01:04:31.730 --> 01:04:35.750
But it's really clear to most people that
we can't get all the way to where we want

01:04:35.750 --> 01:04:38.190
to go just with these flat.

01:04:38.190 --> 01:04:41.960
Because these models, basically they do one
or one and a half things.

01:04:41.960 --> 01:04:44.569
They do pattern recognition and pattern generation.

01:04:44.569 --> 01:04:45.569
That's all.

01:04:45.569 --> 01:04:48.369
They don't do symbolic logic, inductive reasoning.

01:04:48.369 --> 01:04:50.569
Current ones aren't capable of that.

01:04:50.569 --> 01:04:51.809
Tim Ferriss: Irony.

01:04:51.809 --> 01:04:52.809
Tough.

01:04:52.809 --> 01:04:53.809
Kevin Kelly: Irony.

01:04:53.809 --> 01:04:58.309
It's just amazing that they have gone as far
as they have, and we keep expecting that they

01:04:58.309 --> 01:04:59.309
can't go any further.

01:04:59.309 --> 01:05:01.400
But they keep surprising us.

01:05:01.400 --> 01:05:04.660
But we're pretty sure that they can't go all
the way.

01:05:04.660 --> 01:05:06.520
And the example I would use is Wikipedia.

01:05:06.520 --> 01:05:10.000
Wikipedia is a flat, the idea was flat.

01:05:10.000 --> 01:05:11.990
It comes up from the bottom, it's bottom-up.

01:05:11.990 --> 01:05:16.510
It's like how far can you make it reliable,
an encyclopedia just from the bottom-up?

01:05:16.510 --> 01:05:19.670
Well, a lot farther than you would initially
thought.

01:05:19.670 --> 01:05:24.250
But we also know that Wikipedia has succeeded
because in recent years has been more top-down

01:05:24.250 --> 01:05:26.020
control of the editors.

01:05:26.020 --> 01:05:32.430
And you have to have for ultimately what you
want a combination of mostly bottom-up that's

01:05:32.430 --> 01:05:37.140
somewhat regulated by some top-down control,
editorial control, all that kind of stuff.

01:05:37.140 --> 01:05:39.200
And that's what we don't really have in AI
right now.

01:05:39.200 --> 01:05:41.200
We have just the bottom-up.

01:05:41.200 --> 01:05:43.460
It's very, very bottom-up.

01:05:43.460 --> 01:05:48.869
And there is just a suspicion looking at the
Santa Fe work on complex adaptive systems

01:05:48.869 --> 01:05:57.020
that you will want to have some top-down governance
to assist this bottom-up to get where you

01:05:57.020 --> 01:05:58.020
want to go.

01:05:58.020 --> 01:05:59.900
Tim Ferriss: You've interviewed just about
everybody.

01:05:59.900 --> 01:06:01.580
You can get to just about everybody.

01:06:01.580 --> 01:06:08.530
And I know you've spoken to the who's who
of AI and any adjacent field that you want

01:06:08.530 --> 01:06:09.950
to investigate.

01:06:09.950 --> 01:06:11.400
You know lot of people.

01:06:11.400 --> 01:06:12.896
And you know a lot of people that know a lot
of people that know a lot of people.

01:06:12.896 --> 01:06:16.359
So you've spoken to so many.

01:06:16.359 --> 01:06:23.210
And you have, I would say, one of the more
impressive Nostradamus-like predictive track

01:06:23.210 --> 01:06:24.930
records.

01:06:24.930 --> 01:06:31.810
What would be your predictions, speculations
within the next five to 10 years?

01:06:31.810 --> 01:06:35.240
Could be a shorter timeframe of what you think.

01:06:35.240 --> 01:06:36.869
Kevin Kelly: In AI?

01:06:36.869 --> 01:06:38.089
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:06:38.089 --> 01:06:39.309
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:06:39.309 --> 01:06:41.750
So here's what I would expect.

01:06:41.750 --> 01:06:46.880
The thing I want to emphasize is that there's
plural, there's AIs.

01:06:46.880 --> 01:06:53.380
So this idea of the monolithic AI taking over,
they're like machines and they follow the

01:06:53.380 --> 01:06:57.119
general engineering maxim, which is that you
cannot optimize everything.

01:06:57.119 --> 01:06:58.119
There's always trade-offs.

01:06:58.119 --> 01:07:04.869
So we're going to engineer these AIs to be
good for certain things, but not as good as

01:07:04.869 --> 01:07:06.150
something else in another dimension.

01:07:06.150 --> 01:07:09.920
And we already see that with say, the image
generators, some favor artists, some favor

01:07:09.920 --> 01:07:10.920
photography.

01:07:10.920 --> 01:07:13.130
There'll be different personalities.

01:07:13.130 --> 01:07:17.710
The one that does painting the best probably
isn't going to be the best for writing.

01:07:17.710 --> 01:07:22.390
They'll be some kind of transformative —
Tim Ferriss: They're all equally bad at hands,

01:07:22.390 --> 01:07:23.390
I've noticed.

01:07:23.390 --> 01:07:24.390
Kevin Kelly: Right.

01:07:24.390 --> 01:07:26.570
And so I say that the general stance we're
going to have is what I call dumbsmarten.

01:07:26.570 --> 01:07:27.570
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:07:27.570 --> 01:07:29.849
Kevin Kelly: They'll be really smarten.

01:07:29.849 --> 01:07:31.020
Tim Ferriss: Is that Pennsylvanian Dutch?

01:07:31.020 --> 01:07:32.020
Kevin Kelly: I don't know.

01:07:32.020 --> 01:07:33.250
It's kind of like that.

01:07:33.250 --> 01:07:34.290
It's Amish.

01:07:34.290 --> 01:07:35.339
It's dumbsmarten.

01:07:35.339 --> 01:07:36.720
We're just going to be furious.

01:07:36.720 --> 01:07:41.109
It's like, how could you be so dumb when you're
so smart about these other things?

01:07:41.109 --> 01:07:42.799
This is going to be their typical reaction.

01:07:42.799 --> 01:07:46.170
It's like, you're insanely brilliant, but
you're so dumb here.

01:07:46.170 --> 01:07:47.470
Tim Ferriss: Sounds like half of Silicon Valley.

01:07:47.470 --> 01:07:48.470
Kevin Kelly: Exactly.

01:07:48.470 --> 01:07:50.191
dumbsmarten is going to be engineered and
we'll have multiple, so it'll be more and

01:07:50.191 --> 01:07:51.670
more difficult to kind of generalize.

01:07:51.670 --> 01:07:57.119
But what I'm saying is that they're going
to be engineered for specific tasks primarily.

01:07:57.119 --> 01:08:01.980
And there will be a general one, but the general
one would be kind of like the Swiss Army knife.

01:08:01.980 --> 01:08:06.730
It's like good generally, but not really the
best in any one tool.

01:08:06.730 --> 01:08:08.210
That's the engineering maxim.

01:08:08.210 --> 01:08:11.511
And so we should expect multiple varieties
of these.

01:08:11.511 --> 01:08:15.880
And I think the other thing is that they're
the best, for me, the best stance is to think

01:08:15.880 --> 01:08:17.319
of them as artificial aliens.

01:08:17.319 --> 01:08:18.509
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:08:18.509 --> 01:08:22.140
Kevin Kelly: Aliens meaning they could be
like Spock or Yoda.

01:08:22.140 --> 01:08:27.100
Very, very smart, but they're just doing things
differently than we would do.

01:08:27.100 --> 01:08:30.620
If they have a sense of humor, it'll be a
little off.

01:08:30.620 --> 01:08:31.680
But that is actually their benefit.

01:08:31.680 --> 01:08:36.219
The benefit is because they help us think
different, and that's what we're going to

01:08:36.219 --> 01:08:37.219
be using them for.

01:08:37.219 --> 01:08:39.460
That's what people are already using them
for, is generate ideas.

01:08:39.460 --> 01:08:43.670
There's probably an idea that no human would
ever come up with.

01:08:43.670 --> 01:08:46.270
And that helps me come up with a new idea.

01:08:46.270 --> 01:08:51.190
So that stance of — and then the third thing
I would say about the AIs is that most of

01:08:51.190 --> 01:08:52.819
them will be unseen.

01:08:52.819 --> 01:08:55.429
They'll be behind the office operating things.

01:08:55.429 --> 01:08:57.250
The plumbing, the infrastructure.

01:08:57.250 --> 01:09:01.069
And that's actually a sign of their success.

01:09:01.069 --> 01:09:04.290
Technologies succeed when we don't see them
anymore, we don't think about them.

01:09:04.290 --> 01:09:09.279
They become boring and that the majority of
the stuff won't even be outward-facing.

01:09:09.279 --> 01:09:15.179
It'll be just behind the scenes.

01:09:15.179 --> 01:09:19.560
And then this idea of consciousness, consciousness
is a liability.

01:09:19.560 --> 01:09:21.870
You don't want your car to be conscious.

01:09:21.870 --> 01:09:22.870
You want it to drive.

01:09:22.870 --> 01:09:26.659
You don't want to be worrying about whether
it should major in finance, you want it to

01:09:26.659 --> 01:09:28.319
focus on the road.

01:09:28.319 --> 01:09:30.799
So there will be advertising AIs as conscious-free,
right?

01:09:30.799 --> 01:09:33.690
So there are certain —
Tim Ferriss: Dumb and obedient.

01:09:33.690 --> 01:09:34.980
Kevin Kelly: Yes, exactly.

01:09:34.980 --> 01:09:36.909
Tim Ferriss: Extra $30 per month.

01:09:36.909 --> 01:09:37.909
Kevin Kelly: Right.

01:09:37.909 --> 01:09:38.909
Exactly.

01:09:38.909 --> 01:09:40.259
So I think that I would say a couple things.

01:09:40.259 --> 01:09:46.850
One is I think AI overall is underhyped, but
the current version, we won't even call AI

01:09:46.850 --> 01:09:47.850
in 30 years.

01:09:47.850 --> 01:09:49.641
We'll look back and that wasn't it.

01:09:49.641 --> 01:09:52.810
So it means that there's no AI experts right
now.

01:09:52.810 --> 01:09:59.130
So I think, but in the short term, we're probably
overestimating this idea, the vast unemployment

01:09:59.130 --> 01:10:00.130
stuff.

01:10:00.130 --> 01:10:02.140
Not in the next couple of years for sure.

01:10:02.140 --> 01:10:04.560
Tim Ferriss: So everything you've said makes
sense and —

01:10:04.560 --> 01:10:05.560
Kevin Kelly: Right, right.

01:10:05.560 --> 01:10:07.410
Tim Ferriss: Tools will get specialized.

01:10:07.410 --> 01:10:11.900
They will become so embedded that we will
cease to think about them.

01:10:11.900 --> 01:10:12.900
Kevin Kelly: Hopefully.

01:10:12.900 --> 01:10:16.120
Tim Ferriss: Hopefully, right, in the same
way that you waved at the lights, we have

01:10:16.120 --> 01:10:17.440
all sorts of lights in here.

01:10:17.440 --> 01:10:22.500
But it's not like we walk into any room with
artificial light and we think, "Good Lord!"

01:10:22.500 --> 01:10:23.500
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:10:23.500 --> 01:10:25.870
Tim Ferriss: "What is this miracle of engineering?"

01:10:25.870 --> 01:10:26.870
Kevin Kelly: Right, right.

01:10:26.870 --> 01:10:28.600
Tim Ferriss: "And human ingenuity."

01:10:28.600 --> 01:10:33.940
I think most folks would be like, okay, okay,
so why is it underhyped, right?

01:10:33.940 --> 01:10:37.680
What should surprise people or what are people
not appreciating?

01:10:37.680 --> 01:10:40.909
Kevin Kelly: Well, so I was involved with
the internet.

01:10:40.909 --> 01:10:47.510
I was living online for at least 10 years
before 1992, '93 when wires started.

01:10:47.510 --> 01:10:52.810
And in a certain sense, it was like we couldn't
get anybody to just take it seriously.

01:10:52.810 --> 01:10:55.130
It was dismissed as teenage boy stuff.

01:10:55.130 --> 01:10:56.960
And it was kind of that's what it was.

01:10:56.960 --> 01:11:00.890
But I felt like, no, this is really significant.

01:11:00.890 --> 01:11:02.100
This is really powerful.

01:11:02.100 --> 01:11:08.610
And what changed it was an interface change,
became visual for the first time in the web

01:11:08.610 --> 01:11:10.760
was pictures and stuff.

01:11:10.760 --> 01:11:13.540
And that's when everybody switched on.

01:11:13.540 --> 01:11:19.150
Most of the AI happening today has been happening
for years.

01:11:19.150 --> 01:11:21.199
What's new is that we now have an interface.

01:11:21.199 --> 01:11:25.210
We have the conversational, it's this idea
of the large language model.

01:11:25.210 --> 01:11:27.520
We have a conversational interface.

01:11:27.520 --> 01:11:32.880
And that suddenly was oh, the power that's
been there for years is now suddenly accessible.

01:11:32.880 --> 01:11:36.020
It's like having the pictures on the web.

01:11:36.020 --> 01:11:40.250
And so we're suddenly thinking about it, suddenly
in people's faces in the same way that the

01:11:40.250 --> 01:11:41.330
internet was completely fringe.

01:11:41.330 --> 01:11:45.920
And then when the web came along, it very
rapidly became mainstream.

01:11:45.920 --> 01:11:51.719
I remember the first time I saw my gas station,
the pump, there was like a URL.

01:11:51.719 --> 01:11:55.409
I was like "Oh, my gosh, this is like, it's
here."

01:11:55.409 --> 01:12:00.719
I have the same feeling right now happening
with the chat bots and the image generators

01:12:00.719 --> 01:12:04.020
is these capabilities have been around for
at least a decade.

01:12:04.020 --> 01:12:11.139
But now what's new is we have a language interface,
a conversational interface with them.

01:12:11.139 --> 01:12:15.060
And the power's just sort of completely in
our faces now.

01:12:15.060 --> 01:12:17.409
And so where do we go from there?

01:12:17.409 --> 01:12:20.480
I think we are going to then start to apply
this to everything.

01:12:20.480 --> 01:12:25.180
It's going to be, I mean, as we speak every
day there's people embedding this and they're

01:12:25.180 --> 01:12:26.850
going to embed it with this interface.

01:12:26.850 --> 01:12:32.780
So I think we're going to move to this having
a whole 'nother level of interfacing with

01:12:32.780 --> 01:12:35.930
this machine with language.

01:12:35.930 --> 01:12:39.080
And that's very, very powerful.

01:12:39.080 --> 01:12:40.770
We'll just go through the whole thing.

01:12:40.770 --> 01:12:46.710
It's like take X, add the language interface
to it.

01:12:46.710 --> 01:12:48.340
That's really powerful.

01:12:48.340 --> 01:12:56.310
Tim Ferriss: What are you hoping to use AIs
for over the next six to 12 months?

01:12:56.310 --> 01:13:00.080
Kevin Kelly: So first of all, I generate,
post an AI picture every day.

01:13:00.080 --> 01:13:03.190
I have been for —
Tim Ferriss: When did you switch from manual

01:13:03.190 --> 01:13:04.190
to AI?

01:13:04.190 --> 01:13:06.790
Kevin Kelly: Last June, almost a year ago.

01:13:06.790 --> 01:13:10.030
And —
Tim Ferriss: Which tools do you mostly use?

01:13:10.030 --> 01:13:13.949
Kevin Kelly: I tend to go to Midjourney still.

01:13:13.949 --> 01:13:17.949
Midjourney for those who don't, has a very
curious interface.

01:13:17.949 --> 01:13:19.230
It's a Discord channel.

01:13:19.230 --> 01:13:20.230
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:13:20.230 --> 01:13:25.550
Kevin Kelly: And at first I was completely
bamboozled and infuriated with that, but I

01:13:25.550 --> 01:13:27.310
came to see it as genius.

01:13:27.310 --> 01:13:28.870
Tim Ferriss: Why is that?

01:13:28.870 --> 01:13:31.300
Kevin Kelly: Because everybody's working in
the open.

01:13:31.300 --> 01:13:34.850
It's like the ultimate learning vehicle.

01:13:34.850 --> 01:13:36.600
And I learn something every time I go on.

01:13:36.600 --> 01:13:39.610
Tim Ferriss: Being a student in the surgery
theater back in the day.

01:13:39.610 --> 01:13:40.610
Kevin Kelly: Exactly.

01:13:40.610 --> 01:13:42.190
You're seeing how other people do it.

01:13:42.190 --> 01:13:44.480
It's not behind closed doors.

01:13:44.480 --> 01:13:45.980
They're doing it in public.

01:13:45.980 --> 01:13:48.970
And oh, my gosh, you learn so much that way.

01:13:48.970 --> 01:13:49.970
So fast.

01:13:49.970 --> 01:13:54.719
And what's interesting, the year before that,
I did a piece of art every day on my iPad

01:13:54.719 --> 01:13:56.460
with Procreate.

01:13:56.460 --> 01:14:04.020
And I spent almost as much time on the AIs
making an image as I do when I make it myself.

01:14:04.020 --> 01:14:09.690
Because again, the accusation among the painters
in the 1800s when the photography came along

01:14:09.690 --> 01:14:14.820
was, "Oh, you guys, you just push the button."

01:14:14.820 --> 01:14:18.870
And we realize of course now, that photography
is not just pushing the button.

01:14:18.870 --> 01:14:22.690
There's a lot more involved in making a really
great photograph than just pushing the button.

01:14:22.690 --> 01:14:24.870
You have to be in the right position and all
this kind of stuff.

01:14:24.870 --> 01:14:26.420
And the same thing with the AI art.

01:14:26.420 --> 01:14:27.770
It's all, "You're just clicking."

01:14:27.770 --> 01:14:29.449
No, no.

01:14:29.449 --> 01:14:30.449
It's like photography.

01:14:30.449 --> 01:14:34.630
I feel I have some of the same kind of a stance
that I have and I'm photographing, I'm kind

01:14:34.630 --> 01:14:38.590
of hunting, searching through it, I'm trying
to find a good position, a good area where

01:14:38.590 --> 01:14:40.530
there's kind of promise.

01:14:40.530 --> 01:14:44.110
And I'm moving around and I'm trying, and
I'm whispering to the AI, how about this?

01:14:44.110 --> 01:14:45.250
I'm changing the word order.

01:14:45.250 --> 01:14:48.620
I'm actually interacting, having a conversation
with it over time.

01:14:48.620 --> 01:14:54.000
And it might take a half hour or more to get
an image that I am happy with.

01:14:54.000 --> 01:15:00.630
And I'm at that point, very comfortable in
putting my name as a co-creator of it because

01:15:00.630 --> 01:15:05.300
I have, me and the intern have worked together
to make this thing.

01:15:05.300 --> 01:15:10.320
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so you have the art application.

01:15:10.320 --> 01:15:11.320
Any other applications?

01:15:11.320 --> 01:15:12.320
Kevin Kelly: In the future?

01:15:12.320 --> 01:15:13.510
In the next six months.

01:15:13.510 --> 01:15:16.410
I'm actually using the chatbots to help write.

01:15:16.410 --> 01:15:17.520
Tim Ferriss: Chatbots.

01:15:17.520 --> 01:15:18.520
ChatGPT.

01:15:18.520 --> 01:15:21.630
Kevin Kelly: Yes, ChatGPT, Bing, and stuff,
and Google.

01:15:21.630 --> 01:15:24.250
For me, I've always had problems making that
first draft.

01:15:24.250 --> 01:15:25.730
It's just a killer.

01:15:25.730 --> 01:15:26.840
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

01:15:26.840 --> 01:15:27.840
And I know the feeling.

01:15:27.840 --> 01:15:29.239
Kevin Kelly: You know the feeling.

01:15:29.239 --> 01:15:31.420
I find it helpful in making the first draft.

01:15:31.420 --> 01:15:33.320
Tim Ferriss: How do you prompt it?

01:15:33.320 --> 01:15:34.720
What would be an example approach?

01:15:34.720 --> 01:15:37.050
Kevin Kelly: All different ways.

01:15:37.050 --> 01:15:39.159
And I've been collecting this.

01:15:39.159 --> 01:15:41.320
And here's the thing about —
Tim Ferriss: Your book of spells.

01:15:41.320 --> 01:15:42.320
Kevin Kelly: Yes.

01:15:42.320 --> 01:15:46.429
Here's the thing about it, is that this is
an important lesson about technology, is that

01:15:46.429 --> 01:15:49.330
we have to use it to figure it out.

01:15:49.330 --> 01:15:53.590
There's something I call thinkism, which is
this reliance on trying to solve problems

01:15:53.590 --> 01:15:59.409
by thinking about them, which is very appealing
to people who like to think.

01:15:59.409 --> 01:16:04.199
And you can only go so far with thinkism because
all the things we're discovering about this,

01:16:04.199 --> 01:16:08.370
none of the inventors of this had any idea
that they could be used this way.

01:16:08.370 --> 01:16:09.370
Tim Ferriss: That's cool.

01:16:09.370 --> 01:16:10.370
Kevin Kelly: Right?

01:16:10.370 --> 01:16:15.300
And so we're discovering, we collectively,
by using it are discovering its capabilities

01:16:15.300 --> 01:16:18.020
and eventually its harms.

01:16:18.020 --> 01:16:20.719
But that's important because this is how we
steer the things.

01:16:20.719 --> 01:16:25.659
And so the problem with trying to prohibit
or turn it off or ban it, is that you don't

01:16:25.659 --> 01:16:26.659
get to steer anything.

01:16:26.659 --> 01:16:28.280
Going back to that metaphor.

01:16:28.280 --> 01:16:31.580
So right now through use we're uncovering
all these things.

01:16:31.580 --> 01:16:34.679
And I've been trying to track how people are
actually using them.

01:16:34.679 --> 01:16:35.889
For instance, chats.

01:16:35.889 --> 01:16:37.510
There's a couple prompts.

01:16:37.510 --> 01:16:41.929
So here's the thing, these chat models, basically
what they generate are wisdom of the crowd

01:16:41.929 --> 01:16:42.929
kind of knowledge.

01:16:42.929 --> 01:16:47.159
The wisdom of the crowd is very famous, counting
the jelly beans.

01:16:47.159 --> 01:16:52.120
If you average all the attempts by humans
to count the number of the jelly beans in

01:16:52.120 --> 01:16:57.100
a bottle, the best guess, the most accurate,
was the average of it.

01:16:57.100 --> 01:16:59.989
And that's what we're getting with the chat.

01:16:59.989 --> 01:17:05.039
It's taking everything as written, the plus
and the minuses, the geniuses and the jerks,

01:17:05.039 --> 01:17:06.120
and it's averaging out.

01:17:06.120 --> 01:17:08.310
And that's what it's giving you is an average.

01:17:08.310 --> 01:17:16.520
So most of the content generated by the chats
is broadly correct, very average, very bland.

01:17:16.520 --> 01:17:21.719
And a lot of what you're doing with the intern
is pressing them.

01:17:21.719 --> 01:17:26.620
So one of the tricks is that you can ask for
it to be a little bit snarkier or more professional.

01:17:26.620 --> 01:17:32.230
Tim Ferriss: So let's say you're starting
tabula rasa.

01:17:32.230 --> 01:17:34.050
Idea popped into your head in the shower.

01:17:34.050 --> 01:17:36.050
Okay, I want to give a rough draft a shot.

01:17:36.050 --> 01:17:38.850
What is the step number one, step number two?

01:17:38.850 --> 01:17:44.239
Kevin Kelly: So it depends, but I might ask
it to do a summary of what's known about this.

01:17:44.239 --> 01:17:45.660
Tell me everything that it knows about it.

01:17:45.660 --> 01:17:48.760
And then maybe write a first draft with bullets,
five bullet points.

01:17:48.760 --> 01:17:50.100
And then I might —
Tim Ferriss: Could you give a real example

01:17:50.100 --> 01:17:51.360
or a example you might use?

01:17:51.360 --> 01:17:53.560
Kevin Kelly: I'm trying to think of the last
one I did.

01:17:53.560 --> 01:17:58.250
Tim Ferriss: How the Egyptians influenced
Roman architecture.

01:17:58.250 --> 01:17:59.520
I don't know, making that up.

01:17:59.520 --> 01:18:00.520
Kevin Kelly: Exactly right.

01:18:00.520 --> 01:18:01.520
You could do that.

01:18:01.520 --> 01:18:02.520
Give me the five bullet points and stuff.

01:18:02.520 --> 01:18:07.560
And then you could have questions about some
parts you didn't understand or expand bullet

01:18:07.560 --> 01:18:13.270
one citing more sources or give me an example
of a day in the life of this or 10 more examples

01:18:13.270 --> 01:18:15.120
of how this might play out.

01:18:15.120 --> 01:18:16.560
You could expand it that way.

01:18:16.560 --> 01:18:21.199
You could also shrink it in terms of summarizing,
making bullet points or what's the key takeaway

01:18:21.199 --> 01:18:24.800
or how about if I wanted to have a teachable
moment out of this.

01:18:24.800 --> 01:18:28.270
And so you would have all these kinds of things
flowing around.

01:18:28.270 --> 01:18:31.530
And then, again, it's the intern at work.

01:18:31.530 --> 01:18:35.680
It's good but you're not going to use it.

01:18:35.680 --> 01:18:37.141
You're probably rewriting it.

01:18:37.141 --> 01:18:42.350
It maybe gives me some ideas I didn't have
or maybe the structure of how it organized

01:18:42.350 --> 01:18:45.010
it, that's pretty good for four of them.

01:18:45.010 --> 01:18:46.670
And so it's a start.

01:18:46.670 --> 01:18:49.199
And for me —
Tim Ferriss: It gets you past the breath hold

01:18:49.199 --> 01:18:50.199
of the empty page.

01:18:50.199 --> 01:18:53.719
Kevin Kelly: For me, this is really big, this
is getting going.

01:18:53.719 --> 01:18:56.660
And then you can also use it later on.

01:18:56.660 --> 01:19:02.949
I have a friend who writes scripts and they
do, "Show me all the weak plot points in this

01:19:02.949 --> 01:19:03.949
or…"

01:19:03.949 --> 01:19:06.219
Tim Ferriss: Could you explain that?

01:19:06.219 --> 01:19:10.540
Kevin Kelly: You put the script in and they'll
say, "What are some of the contradictory plot

01:19:10.540 --> 01:19:11.540
points?"

01:19:11.540 --> 01:19:12.540
Tim Ferriss: That's a great use.

01:19:12.540 --> 01:19:13.540
Kevin Kelly: Right.

01:19:13.540 --> 01:19:14.540
Just another set of eyes.

01:19:14.540 --> 01:19:17.120
Tim Ferriss: Thinking about fiction, you could
find internal inconsistencies.

01:19:17.120 --> 01:19:21.780
Kevin Kelly: Internal inconsistencies or where's
continuity broken or things like that.

01:19:21.780 --> 01:19:27.469
Or you also could — here's something I used,
my book, which we'll get to, my publisher

01:19:27.469 --> 01:19:29.310
asked for talking points.

01:19:29.310 --> 01:19:36.030
So I said, "Make a list of nine talking points
for this book."

01:19:36.030 --> 01:19:37.890
Tim Ferriss: Time machine number one.

01:19:37.890 --> 01:19:40.280
Woolly mammoth number two.

01:19:40.280 --> 01:19:43.600
Kevin Kelly: It made a good list.

01:19:43.600 --> 01:19:47.639
Again, I didn't, couldn't really use the list,
but I could use it to make the list.

01:19:47.639 --> 01:19:48.639
Tim Ferriss: It was a starting point.

01:19:48.639 --> 01:19:50.440
Kevin Kelly: It was a starting point.

01:19:50.440 --> 01:19:51.440
And here's another thing.

01:19:51.440 --> 01:19:55.800
So I have a friend who has a blog, a daily
blog, they generate 40 posts a day.

01:19:55.800 --> 01:19:58.390
Tim Ferriss: I can probably guess who this
is.

01:19:58.390 --> 01:20:00.070
Kevin Kelly: Probably guess who it is.

01:20:00.070 --> 01:20:01.070
Tim Ferriss: 400?

01:20:01.070 --> 01:20:02.330
I was like, "That's a lot."

01:20:02.330 --> 01:20:04.310
Kevin Kelly: 40 a day.

01:20:04.310 --> 01:20:06.690
So they use it to help write headlines.

01:20:06.690 --> 01:20:09.520
They'll give the thing — they'll say, "Give
me five headlines."

01:20:09.520 --> 01:20:12.940
Tim Ferriss: Now I've actually not used, say,
ChatGPT for this.

01:20:12.940 --> 01:20:17.750
There's an upload function where you can upload
your document and then use that as the basis

01:20:17.750 --> 01:20:20.090
or part of the basis for the prompt.

01:20:20.090 --> 01:20:21.090
Kevin Kelly: Yes.

01:20:21.090 --> 01:20:22.090
Write some headlines.

01:20:22.090 --> 01:20:26.590
He says this is really, really good with suggesting
something that they hadn't thought of.

01:20:26.590 --> 01:20:32.290
Or he said, also, sometimes he'll give it
the posting, and said, "Give me a great punchline

01:20:32.290 --> 01:20:33.290
at the end."

01:20:33.290 --> 01:20:34.850
Tim Ferriss: That's cool.

01:20:34.850 --> 01:20:37.889
Kevin Kelly: And again, it's the intern.

01:20:37.889 --> 01:20:38.889
They're pretty good.

01:20:38.889 --> 01:20:40.411
Tim Ferriss: It's going to give you something,
though.

01:20:40.411 --> 01:20:41.469
Kevin Kelly: Give you something to start with.

01:20:41.469 --> 01:20:45.560
Tim Ferriss: I'll give you an example from
my own life, just for fun.

01:20:45.560 --> 01:20:50.159
A friend of mine suggested this because he
sent me a text, he said, "This is pretty interesting."

01:20:50.159 --> 01:20:55.160
And he used ChatGPT, he's been exploring all
these tools in great depth and I've played

01:20:55.160 --> 01:20:58.929
with a lot of them, but not in the depth that
he has.

01:20:58.929 --> 01:21:06.889
And he had suggested a podcast guest, John
Vervaeke, who was on not too long ago, and

01:21:06.889 --> 01:21:14.639
he simply put in a prompt along the lines
of, "What are questions that Tim Ferriss might

01:21:14.639 --> 01:21:19.260
ask John Vervaeke on his podcast in the style
of Tim Ferriss?"

01:21:19.260 --> 01:21:24.830
And they weren't perfectly polished, but they
were not bad.

01:21:24.830 --> 01:21:26.199
Kevin Kelly: Right?

01:21:26.199 --> 01:21:27.199
Exactly.

01:21:27.199 --> 01:21:29.429
Not bad is, I think, the title.

01:21:29.429 --> 01:21:30.989
I mean, that's what we're talking about.

01:21:30.989 --> 01:21:32.239
It's a not-bad intern.

01:21:32.239 --> 01:21:33.239
Tim Ferriss: Right?

01:21:33.239 --> 01:21:34.239
Kevin Kelly: Okay.

01:21:34.239 --> 01:21:37.580
It's a not-bad intern, it's a universal, not-bad
personal intern.

01:21:37.580 --> 01:21:40.510
They'll get better.

01:21:40.510 --> 01:21:44.500
And I think also, again, they'll get specialized.

01:21:44.500 --> 01:21:55.090
So in 15 years, 15 years — 
so here's my reluctance about that.

01:21:55.090 --> 01:21:59.600
I saw VR for the first time, Jaron Lanier
in 1989, '87.

01:21:59.600 --> 01:22:06.449
I 
could see that in five years.

01:22:06.449 --> 01:22:09.590
I mean, it was so good.

01:22:09.590 --> 01:22:10.590
It was so amazing.

01:22:10.590 --> 01:22:12.699
Tim Ferriss: "We'll be at Ready Player One
in 1992."

01:22:12.699 --> 01:22:14.969
Kevin Kelly: Exactly.

01:22:14.969 --> 01:22:19.290
So what's happened in the 30 years since then?

01:22:19.290 --> 01:22:25.170
The state of the Oculus, whatever, it is about
the same as what I saw with Jaron Lanier.

01:22:25.170 --> 01:22:29.870
But it's a million times cheaper.

01:22:29.870 --> 01:22:32.920
But it really isn't a million times better.

01:22:32.920 --> 01:22:37.550
So I don't know, I think this is the time
for scenarios.

01:22:37.550 --> 01:22:41.340
I think I could have bunch of different scenarios
rather than making a prediction.

01:22:41.340 --> 01:22:42.340
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

01:22:42.340 --> 01:22:43.340
Great.

01:22:43.340 --> 01:22:46.341
Kevin Kelly: And one of the scenarios is that
it doesn't get exponentially better, but it

01:22:46.341 --> 01:22:48.280
just gets exponentially pervasive.

01:22:48.280 --> 01:22:54.200
Tim Ferriss: Which you would think would automatically
— not automatically, that's a strong word,

01:22:54.200 --> 01:22:59.980
would make it better in terms of access to
larger data sets?

01:22:59.980 --> 01:23:03.840
Kevin Kelly: Yes, again, I think one of the
things that might come — we might have a

01:23:03.840 --> 01:23:05.840
much more tailored version of this.

01:23:05.840 --> 01:23:12.960
So going back to your attempts to train it
on your own stuff or to maybe have a more

01:23:12.960 --> 01:23:15.700
customized version of stuff that you want.

01:23:15.700 --> 01:23:21.159
So it's maybe relatively the same level, but
it's much more personalized and tailored and

01:23:21.159 --> 01:23:23.440
customized to what you do and how you do it.

01:23:23.440 --> 01:23:25.750
And also —
Tim Ferriss: Also tailored for my audience.

01:23:25.750 --> 01:23:30.110
And I've already seen a few people do this
because my transcripts are available on the

01:23:30.110 --> 01:23:31.139
website.

01:23:31.139 --> 01:23:39.080
I want to train an AI on my transcripts and
also a lot of material that I produce so that

01:23:39.080 --> 01:23:43.870
the most common questions I get can be sourced
from actual answers I've given.

01:23:43.870 --> 01:23:49.469
Kevin Kelly: So I have a doctor friend who
runs a popular pediatrician site, ask the

01:23:49.469 --> 01:23:56.409
doctor these questions, and he has trained
it on all his 20 years of answering questions

01:23:56.409 --> 01:24:00.870
to his patients to do a chat.

01:24:00.870 --> 01:24:02.930
And it works pretty good.

01:24:02.930 --> 01:24:06.050
And it's, as they say, better than no doctor.

01:24:06.050 --> 01:24:11.040
And that's literally trained on his replies.

01:24:11.040 --> 01:24:15.469
And I think more of that will be coming, even
better.

01:24:15.469 --> 01:24:19.200
So whether we get to the point where we can
do the deductive reasoning of the transfer

01:24:19.200 --> 01:24:23.909
learning, I don't know, because we haven't
really seen that much.

01:24:23.909 --> 01:24:28.309
So that's one scenario, is we suddenly make
another big innovation leap and we have another

01:24:28.309 --> 01:24:32.530
— besides just being able to synthesize
pattern stuff, we can actually do these other

01:24:32.530 --> 01:24:34.159
kinds of cognition.

01:24:34.159 --> 01:24:35.540
That would be huge.

01:24:35.540 --> 01:24:39.869
But there's no evidence that that's going
on.

01:24:39.869 --> 01:24:44.880
So another scenario is just that we have more
of this on a larger scale, more pervasive,

01:24:44.880 --> 01:24:50.510
more tailorised, like in the way that, I don't
know, there's probably been no real advances

01:24:50.510 --> 01:24:54.710
in social media in the 10 years it's been
around.

01:24:54.710 --> 01:24:57.230
Tim Ferriss: All right, excellent advice for
[inaudible].

01:24:57.230 --> 01:25:01.850
Damn it, Kevin, I've been trying to get you
to talk about this and you keep pushing back.

01:25:01.850 --> 01:25:08.290
I was going to segue to this because you mentioned
using AI to think differently, as a catalyst

01:25:08.290 --> 01:25:09.480
for thinking differently.

01:25:09.480 --> 01:25:19.260
And it made me think of advice that I've certainly
read of yours and we've probably had conversations

01:25:19.260 --> 01:25:26.489
about it, which related to career advice for
people, say, in their twenties and exploring

01:25:26.489 --> 01:25:34.389
avenues and creating for themselves or attempting
to create for themselves jobs and activities

01:25:34.389 --> 01:25:37.520
that don't have clear labels.

01:25:37.520 --> 01:25:44.969
And using that, among other tools, as a way
to learn to think differently.

01:25:44.969 --> 01:25:46.000
But let's zoom out.

01:25:46.000 --> 01:25:49.790
So I'm holding two copies of the book.

01:25:49.790 --> 01:25:51.630
One is very, very tiny.

01:25:51.630 --> 01:25:53.080
This is a prized possession, now.

01:25:53.080 --> 01:25:54.820
I've read it probably 12 times.

01:25:54.820 --> 01:25:58.530
I have many, many notes and there are all
sorts of notes in here.

01:25:58.530 --> 01:26:01.139
Excellent Advice for Living.

01:26:01.139 --> 01:26:05.230
Now the tiny version I have says, "Seeds for
contemplation," which I also like.

01:26:05.230 --> 01:26:11.449
And then there's the very beautiful cover
of this galley that you handed to me just

01:26:11.449 --> 01:26:13.380
before we started recording.

01:26:13.380 --> 01:26:17.869
Excellent Advice for Living: Wisdom I Wish
I’d Known Earlier.

01:26:17.869 --> 01:26:18.869
How did this come to be?

01:26:18.869 --> 01:26:20.580
What is the genesis story?

01:26:20.580 --> 01:26:28.950
Kevin Kelly: So I would write down bits of
advice to help me change my own behavior.

01:26:28.950 --> 01:26:34.869
I like to reduce something that I could say
to myself, to repeat to myself, to remember

01:26:34.869 --> 01:26:38.360
something as a way of changing my behavior.

01:26:38.360 --> 01:26:44.810
And that kind of encapsulation and reduction
to a little tiny sentence, for me, it was

01:26:44.810 --> 01:26:47.040
a handle to grab hold of it and bring it forth
when I needed it.

01:26:47.040 --> 01:26:52.040
And an example would be if I know I have something
in my household and I can't find it, then

01:26:52.040 --> 01:26:57.210
when I finally do find it, I'd say to myself,
when I'm getting ready to put it back, "Don't

01:26:57.210 --> 01:27:01.160
put it back where I found it, put it back
where I first looked for it."

01:27:01.160 --> 01:27:05.250
So I had my flashlight, "Put it back where
you first looked for it."

01:27:05.250 --> 01:27:06.250
So I'm reminding myself that.

01:27:06.250 --> 01:27:09.230
And so I would start to write things down.

01:27:09.230 --> 01:27:14.780
Another one would be if I'm invited to do
a talk or go meet somebody or have coffee,

01:27:14.780 --> 01:27:19.199
whatever it is, I would say to myself, "Would
I do this if it was tomorrow morning?"

01:27:19.199 --> 01:27:24.760
As kind of a filter to really make sure it
passed that hurdle, because eventually it

01:27:24.760 --> 01:27:27.780
will be tomorrow morning and I have to think
about it.

01:27:27.780 --> 01:27:29.700
And so I would say, "I got this invite.

01:27:29.700 --> 01:27:31.100
Well that's kind of interesting, good.

01:27:31.100 --> 01:27:34.119
But would I do this tomorrow morning?"

01:27:34.119 --> 01:27:40.369
So making it into some portable way that I
could remind myself very easily.

01:27:40.369 --> 01:27:45.190
And I start to get in the habit of writing
these down and I realized a lot of it was

01:27:45.190 --> 01:27:48.420
advice that I wish I had known earlier.

01:27:48.420 --> 01:27:49.739
I have three kids.

01:27:49.739 --> 01:27:55.369
And the way of our parenting was the opposite
of helicopter parenting, was very hands off.

01:27:55.369 --> 01:27:59.280
And we did not give them much advice ever.

01:27:59.280 --> 01:28:03.870
When I was growing up, I didn't really pay
attention to what my parents said, I paid

01:28:03.870 --> 01:28:05.300
attention to what they did.

01:28:05.300 --> 01:28:07.469
And I figured that's what our kids were doing
too.

01:28:07.469 --> 01:28:10.850
So we try to model behavior rather than to
say it.

01:28:10.850 --> 01:28:18.429
So neither my wife or I have ever really gave
much advice, but I thought that now that I

01:28:18.429 --> 01:28:20.900
was writing it down that I should give them
advice.

01:28:20.900 --> 01:28:26.260
So I began this idea of trying to extract
out and put into a little handle of something

01:28:26.260 --> 01:28:30.059
encapsulated that I could give to my kids.

01:28:30.059 --> 01:28:32.550
I did a bunch when I was 68.

01:28:32.550 --> 01:28:38.810
On my birthday I released it to my son at
that time, who was a young adult, just becoming

01:28:38.810 --> 01:28:39.969
a young adult.

01:28:39.969 --> 01:28:44.219
And a lot of people loved it and it ricocheted
around the internet.

01:28:44.219 --> 01:28:48.719
I did it for a couple more years and I realized
that I had a lot to say.

01:28:48.719 --> 01:28:52.119
I was finally giving advice that I'd been
giving myself —

01:28:52.119 --> 01:28:54.159
Tim Ferriss: Bottled up for all these decades.

01:28:54.159 --> 01:28:55.439
Kevin Kelly: — for 70 years.

01:28:55.439 --> 01:28:57.469
I was giving it out.

01:28:57.469 --> 01:28:59.909
But it's scattered around the internet.

01:28:59.909 --> 01:29:02.239
And I thought it would be really handy to
have it in a book.

01:29:02.239 --> 01:29:06.780
So I made a prototype myself, just made a
little book.

01:29:06.780 --> 01:29:12.409
I made five copies and I sent it around to
see if it worked as a book.

01:29:12.409 --> 01:29:16.020
And this book also has my little doodles in
it.

01:29:16.020 --> 01:29:17.369
And it worked.

01:29:17.369 --> 01:29:19.219
And so I sent it to a publisher, they loved
it.

01:29:19.219 --> 01:29:24.400
They said they didn't like the doodles, and
so they said, "No art by you."

01:29:24.400 --> 01:29:29.619
So it's in a portable form.

01:29:29.619 --> 01:29:35.850
And actually I realized afterwards, although
it was not in my head at the time, but it's

01:29:35.850 --> 01:29:37.030
very tweetable.

01:29:37.030 --> 01:29:38.030
These are tweets.

01:29:38.030 --> 01:29:43.710
And so they work at the attention span of
a young person these days and they transmit

01:29:43.710 --> 01:29:44.710
well.

01:29:44.710 --> 01:29:48.469
And so the version you have here has about
another hundred that aren't even in this —

01:29:48.469 --> 01:29:50.030
Tim Ferriss: Oh, look at this.

01:29:50.030 --> 01:29:54.869
So just when I thought Christmas was far away.

01:29:54.869 --> 01:30:01.480
Kevin Kelly: I tried to make them as practical
as possible, actionable, not conventional,

01:30:01.480 --> 01:30:07.680
positive, if at all possible, and short.

01:30:07.680 --> 01:30:13.560
"You can find no better medicine for your
family than regular meals together without

01:30:13.560 --> 01:30:14.560
screens."

01:30:14.560 --> 01:30:20.030
Tim Ferriss: Let me throw a few out just because
I have so many notes in here.

01:30:20.030 --> 01:30:27.429
So I'll mention a couple and one that I may
not get verbatim, but I've thought quite a

01:30:27.429 --> 01:30:31.670
bit about, because when I look back at all
the places I've spent time, it's totally true.

01:30:31.670 --> 01:30:37.010
It's something like if an outdoor patio is
less than six feet wide, no one will ever

01:30:37.010 --> 01:30:38.010
use it.

01:30:38.010 --> 01:30:39.010
Kevin Kelly: A balcony.

01:30:39.010 --> 01:30:40.010
No one will use a balcony unless it's —
Tim Ferriss: Never, ever.

01:30:40.010 --> 01:30:41.010
Kevin Kelly: Never.

01:30:41.010 --> 01:30:43.030
Tim Ferriss: And I thought about it, I was
like, "That a hundred percent maps to all

01:30:43.030 --> 01:30:44.730
of my experience."

01:30:44.730 --> 01:30:49.369
So we have, "What you do on your bad days
matters more than what you do on your good

01:30:49.369 --> 01:30:50.369
days."

01:30:50.369 --> 01:30:58.410
This also overlaps with a lot of my thoughts
on ritual and routine versus relying on, say,

01:30:58.410 --> 01:30:59.410
discipline.

01:30:59.410 --> 01:31:03.630
"Greatness is incompatible with optimizing
in the short term," and it goes on and on.

01:31:03.630 --> 01:31:06.230
I'm just mentioning a few that I've highlighted
for myself.

01:31:06.230 --> 01:31:08.760
"You don't marry a person, you marry a family."

01:31:08.760 --> 01:31:09.949
That's a big one.

01:31:09.949 --> 01:31:14.730
Kevin Kelly: I decided you don't marry a family,
you marry a country because my wife is from

01:31:14.730 --> 01:31:15.730
a different country.

01:31:15.730 --> 01:31:19.780
Tim Ferriss: And who knows, maybe a hundred
years from now you don't marry a person, you

01:31:19.780 --> 01:31:21.820
marry a planet, a species.

01:31:21.820 --> 01:31:24.889
"If you can't tell what you desperately need,
it's probably sleep."

01:31:24.889 --> 01:31:30.970
"Don't aim to have others like you, aim to
have them respect you," which also helps in

01:31:30.970 --> 01:31:34.250
saying no to things that you won't want to
do tomorrow morning.

01:31:34.250 --> 01:31:37.760
Are there any that come to mind for you — here
it is, "A balcony or porch needs to be at

01:31:37.760 --> 01:31:40.429
least six feet deep or it won't be used."

01:31:40.429 --> 01:31:44.659
Then there are some very, very specific recommendations.

01:31:44.659 --> 01:31:47.360
"Learn to tie a bowline knot.

01:31:47.360 --> 01:31:49.430
Practice in the dark with one hand for the
rest of your life.

01:31:49.430 --> 01:31:52.020
You'll use this knot more times than you could
ever believe."

01:31:52.020 --> 01:31:53.020
True.

01:31:53.020 --> 01:32:01.250
I only learned that knot, I want to say, in
2012, and I've used it a million times since.

01:32:01.250 --> 01:32:06.980
What are some here that people have responded
particularly well to?

01:32:06.980 --> 01:32:10.659
Are there any that pop out, that come to mind?

01:32:10.659 --> 01:32:13.250
I'll buy you some time.

01:32:13.250 --> 01:32:17.369
"When you feel pressure to pick a choice,
don't forget the choice of not choosing any."

01:32:17.369 --> 01:32:18.469
That's one I starred for myself.

01:32:18.469 --> 01:32:22.369
Kevin Kelly: One thing that has surprised
me is that there've been no overlap in people's

01:32:22.369 --> 01:32:24.760
favorites.

01:32:24.760 --> 01:32:27.119
No overlaps in people's favorites.

01:32:27.119 --> 01:32:28.310
I don't know what to make of that.

01:32:28.310 --> 01:32:29.310
But anyway, that's what it is.

01:32:29.310 --> 01:32:32.430
So I'll tell you some of my favorites.

01:32:32.430 --> 01:32:38.639
When you're in your twenties you should spend
a little bit of time doing something that's

01:32:38.639 --> 01:32:49.429
sort of crazy, insane, unprofitable, unorthodox,
orthogonal, because that's going to be your

01:32:49.429 --> 01:32:54.140
touchstone and the foundation of your success
later on.

01:32:54.140 --> 01:32:58.300
Try and deliberately — don't try for something
successful or creative.

01:32:58.300 --> 01:33:02.330
Do something very, very strange and weird.

01:33:02.330 --> 01:33:06.719
It's kind of like to the other bit of advice
of the thing that made you weird as a kid

01:33:06.719 --> 01:33:08.969
can make you great as an adult if you don't
lose it.

01:33:08.969 --> 01:33:09.969
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

01:33:09.969 --> 01:33:11.490
Kevin Kelly: I mean, this is very true.

01:33:11.490 --> 01:33:12.490
Tim Ferriss: It's true.

01:33:12.490 --> 01:33:13.490
It's very true.

01:33:13.490 --> 01:33:14.490
Very true.

01:33:14.490 --> 01:33:18.230
Kevin Kelly: So one of my favorite bits of
advice that can be expanded, which is, "Don't

01:33:18.230 --> 01:33:19.739
aim to be the best.

01:33:19.739 --> 01:33:21.230
Be the only."

01:33:21.230 --> 01:33:23.080
Tim Ferriss: Category of one.

01:33:23.080 --> 01:33:24.179
Kevin Kelly: Here's the category of one.

01:33:24.179 --> 01:33:26.490
Right before us you are living that.

01:33:26.490 --> 01:33:29.450
Even though if 10 years ago, I don't know
when you started your podcast —

01:33:29.450 --> 01:33:30.699
Tim Ferriss: Almost 10.

01:33:30.699 --> 01:33:31.699
It'll be 10 years next year.

01:33:31.699 --> 01:33:34.080
Kevin Kelly: You want to be doing something
where it's hard to explain to your mother

01:33:34.080 --> 01:33:35.409
what it is that you do.

01:33:35.409 --> 01:33:37.990
So it's like, "What is it?

01:33:37.990 --> 01:33:40.050
Well, it's not quite radio.

01:33:40.050 --> 01:33:41.050
I don't know.

01:33:41.050 --> 01:33:42.510
It's like talking."

01:33:42.510 --> 01:33:44.590
And so that's where you want to be.

01:33:44.590 --> 01:33:45.590
You want to be the only.

01:33:45.590 --> 01:33:49.570
You want to — and that's a very high bar
because it requires a tremendous amount of

01:33:49.570 --> 01:33:53.510
self-knowledge and awareness to get to that
point, to really understand what it is that

01:33:53.510 --> 01:33:56.270
you do better than anybody else in the world.

01:33:56.270 --> 01:34:00.160
And for most of us, it takes all our lives
to figure that out.

01:34:00.160 --> 01:34:09.199
And we also, by the way, need family, friends,
colleagues, customers, clients, everyone around

01:34:09.199 --> 01:34:13.989
us to help us understand what it is that we
do better than anybody else because we can't

01:34:13.989 --> 01:34:15.239
really get there by yourself.

01:34:15.239 --> 01:34:20.620
You can't do thinkism, you can't figure your
way there, you have to try and live it out.

01:34:20.620 --> 01:34:25.619
And that's why most people's remarkable lives
are full of detours and dead ends and right

01:34:25.619 --> 01:34:29.100
turns because it's a very high bar.

01:34:29.100 --> 01:34:34.940
But if you can get there — you don't need
a resume, there's no competition.

01:34:34.940 --> 01:34:38.340
And it's easy for you because you're doing
it.

01:34:38.340 --> 01:34:41.780
You're not looking over your shoulder, you're
just right there.

01:34:41.780 --> 01:34:44.760
So don't aim to be the best.

01:34:44.760 --> 01:34:45.760
Be the only.

01:34:45.760 --> 01:34:51.560
Tim Ferriss: On the easy front, a question
that I've sometimes asked my friends because

01:34:51.560 --> 01:34:54.300
the things that are right in front of us all
the time are sometimes the hardest to see

01:34:54.300 --> 01:34:55.350
—
Kevin Kelly: There you go.

01:34:55.350 --> 01:34:59.650
Tim Ferriss: — is what do you think is easy
for me that is harder for other people?

01:34:59.650 --> 01:35:03.480
Because sometimes you take, I shouldn't say
— oftentimes take it for granted because

01:35:03.480 --> 01:35:08.989
it's just what you do and you don't even see
where you have something that falls into that

01:35:08.989 --> 01:35:09.989
category.

01:35:09.989 --> 01:35:14.880
Kevin Kelly: I actually use that, in a similar
form of the question, at dinner parties when

01:35:14.880 --> 01:35:16.030
I'm sitting next to a buddy.

01:35:16.030 --> 01:35:22.650
So one of my bits of advice is that almost
everybody knows a lot more about something

01:35:22.650 --> 01:35:23.951
than anybody else around them.

01:35:23.951 --> 01:35:29.290
And so I'll sit down, it's like, "What do
you know more about than most people?"

01:35:29.290 --> 01:35:32.880
And I feel it's my job to find out what that
is.

01:35:32.880 --> 01:35:38.580
And it's not obvious, it's not always obvious,
you have to work at it, but it's always amazing.

01:35:38.580 --> 01:35:40.010
Tim Ferriss: Yes, totally.

01:35:40.010 --> 01:35:45.690
Kevin Kelly: If you can get there, they know
something that'll just blow your mind.

01:35:45.690 --> 01:35:49.530
And so that's my assignment when I go to a
party.

01:35:49.530 --> 01:35:54.230
That person knows an amazing amount of something,
but it's not going to be obvious, but they'll

01:35:54.230 --> 01:35:55.230
tell me.

01:35:55.230 --> 01:36:01.750
Tim Ferriss: I have a close cousin of that
that I sometimes use myself, which is once

01:36:01.750 --> 01:36:06.840
someone has shared, or maybe it's already
known what they do professionally, their primary

01:36:06.840 --> 01:36:10.590
gig, let's just say it's finance in some capacity.

01:36:10.590 --> 01:36:14.249
And I might just ask — could be anything
though, doesn't matter.

01:36:14.249 --> 01:36:19.100
And I would just ask, "If you had to give
a TED Talk, 20 minutes long, but it couldn't

01:36:19.100 --> 01:36:23.650
be on anything that people at this table know
you for, including finance, what would it

01:36:23.650 --> 01:36:24.650
be on?"

01:36:24.650 --> 01:36:30.990
And you get some of the most out-of-left-field
responses and it opens the floodgates to a

01:36:30.990 --> 01:36:31.990
really interesting conversation.

01:36:31.990 --> 01:36:33.320
I'm going to mention a couple more.

01:36:33.320 --> 01:36:36.480
"Your enjoyment of travel is inversely proportional
to the size of your luggage."

01:36:36.480 --> 01:36:38.550
This is 100 percent true of backpacking.

01:36:38.550 --> 01:36:40.949
It is liberating to realize how little you
really need.

01:36:40.949 --> 01:36:45.320
And if people are not familiar with our first
conversation, they should listen to it and

01:36:45.320 --> 01:36:49.140
they will realize that you walk the walk with
that.

01:36:49.140 --> 01:36:53.449
You have certainly walked the walk with that
and are minimalist in so many ways.

01:36:53.449 --> 01:36:55.400
I want to ask a follow-up question to one
of these.

01:36:55.400 --> 01:36:59.239
"For the best results with your children,"
I starred this one, "Spend only half the money

01:36:59.239 --> 01:37:02.699
you think you should, but double the time
with them."

01:37:02.699 --> 01:37:08.739
One of the letters, I don't get a lot of physical
letters, but one of the letters that I most

01:37:08.739 --> 01:37:13.869
look forward to every year, I think it's your
year in review or Christmas perhaps, but you

01:37:13.869 --> 01:37:18.989
give a recap and you talk about the family
and adventures and so on.

01:37:18.989 --> 01:37:25.550
What have you found to be some of the best
investments of time with your kids?

01:37:25.550 --> 01:37:31.590
Because abstractly I bet most parents would
nod their heads and say, "Of course, that

01:37:31.590 --> 01:37:32.659
makes sense.

01:37:32.659 --> 01:37:33.659
Absolutely."

01:37:33.659 --> 01:37:39.139
But not all ways of spending time with kids
are equal, I would imagine.

01:37:39.139 --> 01:37:45.389
For building intimacy and a relationship with
your kids or maybe other things, what have

01:37:45.389 --> 01:37:47.340
you found to be really good uses of time?

01:37:47.340 --> 01:37:51.230
Kevin Kelly: I don't know enough about this
generally, but I can only tell you what we

01:37:51.230 --> 01:37:52.230
have done ourselves.

01:37:52.230 --> 01:37:54.110
And for me —
Tim Ferriss: That's what I'm asking.

01:37:54.110 --> 01:37:57.410
Kevin Kelly: For me, what I've seen is a couple
things.

01:37:57.410 --> 01:38:00.989
One is making things together.

01:38:00.989 --> 01:38:07.900
I'm a maker and so I have to work against
my better instincts to be too involved or

01:38:07.900 --> 01:38:13.540
take over too much, but to really allow that
cooperative, joint, making together.

01:38:13.540 --> 01:38:17.030
And it's fun for the kid and it's fun for
me.

01:38:17.030 --> 01:38:21.930
And you get to see frustrations, overcoming
frustrations, making mistakes, overcoming

01:38:21.930 --> 01:38:22.930
mistakes.

01:38:22.930 --> 01:38:27.830
So there's a tremendous amount going on, both
of them learning and me learning about them.

01:38:27.830 --> 01:38:29.370
And that togetherness is really great.

01:38:29.370 --> 01:38:31.250
Tim Ferriss: What kind of stuff have you made
together?

01:38:31.250 --> 01:38:37.389
Kevin Kelly: We've made go karts, we've made
gingerbread castles, we've made Styrobot.

01:38:37.389 --> 01:38:40.680
I don't know if you — one of my all time
favorites was making Styrobot with my son.

01:38:40.680 --> 01:38:41.680
Tim Ferriss: I remember Styrobot.

01:38:41.680 --> 01:38:45.140
Kevin Kelly: It was made from recycled styrofoam.

01:38:45.140 --> 01:38:47.199
It's nine feet tall.

01:38:47.199 --> 01:38:55.280
Just art projects, helping in the garden,
doing chores together.

01:38:55.280 --> 01:39:03.180
I would say travel is undoubtedly one of the
best learning experiences, so much that I

01:39:03.180 --> 01:39:07.719
think as a nation we should subsidize travel,
but as a family, if you can afford it.

01:39:07.719 --> 01:39:12.239
And I don't mean that it's going on vacation,
the kind of travel I like is where you are

01:39:12.239 --> 01:39:13.239
learning.

01:39:13.239 --> 01:39:19.719
It's like a learning experience, it's an experience
and oh, my gosh, is that so powerful.

01:39:19.719 --> 01:39:24.060
And I would say one thing about it, that I've
learned doing it, and that is that I was a

01:39:24.060 --> 01:39:29.760
little concerned when we were doing very intense
travel with my kids at a younger age, they,

01:39:29.760 --> 01:39:34.060
at times, didn't seem to be paying attention.

01:39:34.060 --> 01:39:38.750
They seemed not to be aware, or they were
maybe wanting to stay in the hotel and play

01:39:38.750 --> 01:39:42.489
cards or things like that when we were in
Tibet, whatever.

01:39:42.489 --> 01:39:48.290
But later on I found out that actually they
were paying attention, but they couldn't process

01:39:48.290 --> 01:39:50.240
it at the time.

01:39:50.240 --> 01:39:54.040
So the experience was there and they would
reprocess it over time and it would become

01:39:54.040 --> 01:39:58.469
more and more valuable as they had more and
more to interpret it.

01:39:58.469 --> 01:40:02.800
And that trip, which I thought was a failure
at the time, because they were not really

01:40:02.800 --> 01:40:07.070
appreciating it, but actually grew in importance.

01:40:07.070 --> 01:40:10.990
And so don't be dismayed if you take your
kids on a great adventure and they're kind

01:40:10.990 --> 01:40:14.080
of not impressed or they're not changed or
whatever.

01:40:14.080 --> 01:40:15.080
No, no, no.

01:40:15.080 --> 01:40:17.920
They haven't yet been able to process it and
they'll process it over time.

01:40:17.920 --> 01:40:21.870
Tim Ferriss: Any other tips for traveling
with kids?

01:40:21.870 --> 01:40:29.770
Because I can imagine a family trip that it
is, well, certainly very different from a

01:40:29.770 --> 01:40:34.280
Kevin Kelly family trip where a family has
gone to five countries, they've stayed in

01:40:34.280 --> 01:40:38.650
the Four Seasons in each one, the kids have
been on their phones the entire time.

01:40:38.650 --> 01:40:40.870
And you can fill in the rest of the picture.

01:40:40.870 --> 01:40:43.520
So what would be perhaps some recommendations?

01:40:43.520 --> 01:40:48.320
And I'm not saying people should sleep on
the sidewalks, just you can travel and not

01:40:48.320 --> 01:40:51.989
actually leave the comfort of your own usual
scaffolding.

01:40:51.989 --> 01:40:53.239
Kevin Kelly: Yes, no.

01:40:53.239 --> 01:40:58.989
One of the bits of advice is like, "A vacation
plus a disaster equals an adventure."

01:40:58.989 --> 01:41:04.599
One of the times I rode my bicycle across
the US was from Vancouver down to Mexico on

01:41:04.599 --> 01:41:05.599
the coast.

01:41:05.599 --> 01:41:11.730
And I rode with my son who was a teenager,
and I had his nephew who was a teenager along.

01:41:11.730 --> 01:41:14.739
That was an incredible experience.

01:41:14.739 --> 01:41:21.720
And doing that together, like an adventure
together, was tremendous for them because

01:41:21.720 --> 01:41:25.829
it took them out of the San Francisco bubble
and they could see the real world.

01:41:25.829 --> 01:41:32.670
And again, it was a learning experience about
overcoming, doing things that seemed hard,

01:41:32.670 --> 01:41:36.010
but turned out to be things that you just
could do.

01:41:36.010 --> 01:41:41.470
So I would say yes, try new things.

01:41:41.470 --> 01:41:51.500
Another thing that I think is really good
for travel is to almost go somewhere at random.

01:41:51.500 --> 01:41:52.500
I mean literally.

01:41:52.500 --> 01:41:58.349
So our policy for our family was to follow
passions and interests rather than destinations.

01:41:58.349 --> 01:42:04.580
So every kid, we would say, "We're going to
follow your passion for our vacation this

01:42:04.580 --> 01:42:05.580
year.

01:42:05.580 --> 01:42:06.580
Whatever you're interested in."

01:42:06.580 --> 01:42:09.590
If you were interested Ann of Green Gables,
okay, we're going to go to Nova Scotia and

01:42:09.590 --> 01:42:11.710
visit all the Anne of Green Gables sites.

01:42:11.710 --> 01:42:13.710
If your kid —
Tim Ferriss: What is Anne of Green Gables?

01:42:13.710 --> 01:42:14.710
I probably should know.

01:42:14.710 --> 01:42:19.100
Kevin Kelly: Anne of Green Gables is a popular
children's or young adults' story.

01:42:19.100 --> 01:42:20.100
Tim Ferriss: I see.

01:42:20.100 --> 01:42:21.680
Kevin Kelly: Anne of Green Gables.

01:42:21.680 --> 01:42:27.260
And there's a series of books and it was based
in Nova Scotia and there's kind of a big following

01:42:27.260 --> 01:42:28.260
about that.

01:42:28.260 --> 01:42:29.260
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

01:42:29.260 --> 01:42:31.199
Kevin Kelly: So our son was interested in
dinosaurs when he was very little.

01:42:31.199 --> 01:42:32.199
Okay.

01:42:32.199 --> 01:42:37.610
We're going to go to all the dinosaur digs
and dinosaur museums and dinosaur excavations

01:42:37.610 --> 01:42:38.989
we could find.

01:42:38.989 --> 01:42:39.989
And that was the theme.

01:42:39.989 --> 01:42:41.830
And they were very engaged in that vacation.

01:42:41.830 --> 01:42:46.310
We were in an RV and we're going around visiting
dinosaurs because that was — so the child

01:42:46.310 --> 01:42:47.310
got to pick.

01:42:47.310 --> 01:42:54.780
Tim Ferriss: So the follow-up question I had
after the bicycle story was whether your kids

01:42:54.780 --> 01:43:04.440
have always been game for these adventures,
or whether you've cultivated/Jedi mind-tricked

01:43:04.440 --> 01:43:07.160
them into being more game?

01:43:07.160 --> 01:43:10.430
Because I would have to imagine, this is speculation,
but there are parents listening and they're

01:43:10.430 --> 01:43:11.940
like, "I could never convince my teenage…"

01:43:11.940 --> 01:43:12.940
Kevin Kelly: Oh, I see.

01:43:12.940 --> 01:43:16.880
Tim Ferriss: "...son to go cycling for weeks
at a time or months at a time," whatever it

01:43:16.880 --> 01:43:17.880
might be.

01:43:17.880 --> 01:43:19.030
And maybe that's just out of the box —
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:43:19.030 --> 01:43:20.080
No —
Tim Ferriss: — they've been ready to go.

01:43:20.080 --> 01:43:22.860
Kevin Kelly: I guess I would say, it was not
an issue that we had.

01:43:22.860 --> 01:43:27.610
Yeah, they were always up for it, particularly,
again, if you have something that's surrounding

01:43:27.610 --> 01:43:28.610
their own passion.

01:43:28.610 --> 01:43:29.845
Tim Ferriss: I was imagining that if you've
— what's the right term?

01:43:29.845 --> 01:43:36.270
If you folded them into the process of making
these decisions from an early age, they relate

01:43:36.270 --> 01:43:38.660
to these things differently than if —
Kevin Kelly: Exactly.

01:43:38.660 --> 01:43:39.660
Tim Ferriss: — everything has been an assignment.

01:43:39.660 --> 01:43:40.660
Kevin Kelly: Right.

01:43:40.660 --> 01:43:41.660
So I think that's how you do it.

01:43:41.660 --> 01:43:42.660
It is an extension.

01:43:42.660 --> 01:43:47.020
And we homeschooled our son for one year and
part of that home year of homeschooling, we

01:43:47.020 --> 01:43:48.150
weren't home, we were traveling.

01:43:48.150 --> 01:43:52.840
And again, it was this idea of, "Where would
you like to go?

01:43:52.840 --> 01:43:56.409
You get to set some of the things."

01:43:56.409 --> 01:43:57.409
And that was total engagement.

01:43:57.409 --> 01:43:59.020
You want to make sure that it's —
Tim Ferriss: You're invested.

01:43:59.020 --> 01:44:00.020
Kevin Kelly: Yeah, you're invested.

01:44:00.020 --> 01:44:01.020
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:44:01.020 --> 01:44:02.020
I'm going to grab a few more here.

01:44:02.020 --> 01:44:07.619
"If an elementary school student is struggling,
first thing, check their eyesight."

01:44:07.619 --> 01:44:11.909
Good advice, something I need to get checked
'cause my family has quite a bit of glaucoma

01:44:11.909 --> 01:44:15.730
and intraocular pressure issues and I haven't
had them checked in a long time, so I'll get

01:44:15.730 --> 01:44:16.730
there.

01:44:16.730 --> 01:44:20.270
"Purchase the most recent tourist guidebook
to your hometown or region.

01:44:20.270 --> 01:44:22.449
You'll learn a lot by playing the tourist
once a year."

01:44:22.449 --> 01:44:23.449
This is something I did.

01:44:23.449 --> 01:44:26.199
I went out and I actually got the guide books.

01:44:26.199 --> 01:44:27.680
And you do pick up a lot.

01:44:27.680 --> 01:44:28.680
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:44:28.680 --> 01:44:29.680
Yeah.

01:44:29.680 --> 01:44:30.680
Tim Ferriss: You pick up a lot.

01:44:30.680 --> 01:44:31.680
I did this in San Francisco too.

01:44:31.680 --> 01:44:32.680
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:44:32.680 --> 01:44:33.680
Yeah.

01:44:33.680 --> 01:44:34.680
Tim Ferriss: It's shocking how much you miss.

01:44:34.680 --> 01:44:35.680
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:44:35.680 --> 01:44:39.739
Tim Ferriss: I remember growing up in New
York, I never made it to the Statue of Liberty

01:44:39.739 --> 01:44:46.550
or the Empire State Building until a German
friend visited me 10 years ago and was shocked

01:44:46.550 --> 01:44:48.380
that I had never been to either of these things.

01:44:48.380 --> 01:44:53.530
And I was like, "You know, maybe I should
at least spend an afternoon and go see these

01:44:53.530 --> 01:44:54.530
things."

01:44:54.530 --> 01:44:55.530
And it was a blast.

01:44:55.530 --> 01:44:56.530
It was a lot of fun.

01:44:56.530 --> 01:44:59.389
Kevin Kelly: One Thanksgiving day we went
into San Francisco and rode the cable cars,

01:44:59.389 --> 01:45:02.000
which we had never been on.

01:45:02.000 --> 01:45:06.730
And it was the perfect day because there was
nobody else on them for Thanksgiving day.

01:45:06.730 --> 01:45:14.660
And so, yes, it's a way of, again, I favor
things to help you learn, give you new experience,

01:45:14.660 --> 01:45:19.770
and treating your own neighborhood is a great
way to do that.

01:45:19.770 --> 01:45:23.380
Tim Ferriss: So I'm giving some more — I
could keep going 'cause there's so many good

01:45:23.380 --> 01:45:24.380
ones.

01:45:24.380 --> 01:45:26.290
But I'll give an example of highly tactical
and specific, all right.

01:45:26.290 --> 01:45:30.090
"To signal an emergency, use the rule of three:
Three shouts, three horn blasts, or three

01:45:30.090 --> 01:45:31.260
whistles."

01:45:31.260 --> 01:45:35.760
And then you have the more, I would say, still
practical, but conceptual.

01:45:35.760 --> 01:45:39.120
"When you're stuck, explain your problem to
others.

01:45:39.120 --> 01:45:41.830
Often simply laying out a problem will present
a solution.

01:45:41.830 --> 01:45:45.310
Make 'explaining the problem' part of your
troubleshooting process."

01:45:45.310 --> 01:45:48.210
And I —
Kevin Kelly: I wish I knew that so much earlier.

01:45:48.210 --> 01:45:54.090
Tim Ferriss: I would not have been able to
write The 4-Hour Workweek had I not taken

01:45:54.090 --> 01:45:55.349
this advice.

01:45:55.349 --> 01:45:58.610
I was totally stuck on an entire section.

01:45:58.610 --> 01:46:02.139
I was roadblocked, couldn't make any progress.

01:46:02.139 --> 01:46:08.679
I was really starting to panic and a writer
friend of mine said, "Just have someone interview

01:46:08.679 --> 01:46:12.300
you about why you're having trouble and record
it."

01:46:12.300 --> 01:46:17.489
And I did that, I had somebody who was a ghost
writer, I've never used a ghost writer, but

01:46:17.489 --> 01:46:19.449
she knew how to ask questions.

01:46:19.449 --> 01:46:25.060
Recorded the conversation, didn't even need
the recording because it helped me walk right

01:46:25.060 --> 01:46:26.060
through it.

01:46:26.060 --> 01:46:27.659
And by the end, I knew what the solution was.

01:46:27.659 --> 01:46:28.659
Problem solved.

01:46:28.659 --> 01:46:29.659
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:46:29.659 --> 01:46:35.970
Well, I have an assistant and when I get stuck,
I'll start to explain to her my thing.

01:46:35.970 --> 01:46:39.489
And then by the end it's like, "Wait a minute,
I get it."

01:46:39.489 --> 01:46:41.030
I was like, "I didn't need you.

01:46:41.030 --> 01:46:43.680
But actually I did need you just to walk you
through it."

01:46:43.680 --> 01:46:44.680
Yeah.

01:46:44.680 --> 01:46:45.680
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:46:45.680 --> 01:46:47.450
What would you hope — I'm going to ask you
the same question I asked about — is it

01:46:47.450 --> 01:46:49.480
The Silver Cord or —
Kevin Kelly: The Silver Cord.

01:46:49.480 --> 01:46:50.480
Tim Ferriss: It is.

01:46:50.480 --> 01:46:52.200
I just wanted to make sure I was adding the
article or not.

01:46:52.200 --> 01:46:55.820
So The Silver Cord, what would you hope this
to do?

01:46:55.820 --> 01:46:58.320
What would be success for you?

01:46:58.320 --> 01:46:59.389
Or is it already successful?

01:46:59.389 --> 01:47:00.640
You're like, "Eh, I've done it."

01:47:00.640 --> 01:47:03.350
Kevin Kelly: Generally my books have an audience
of one.

01:47:03.350 --> 01:47:05.719
I did this big Asia book.

01:47:05.719 --> 01:47:06.719
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:47:06.719 --> 01:47:10.369
Kevin Kelly: 30 pounds, three volumes —
Tim Ferriss: Spectacularly, beautiful, gorgeous,

01:47:10.369 --> 01:47:11.650
gorgeous, gorgeous book.

01:47:11.650 --> 01:47:13.659
Kevin Kelly: — 9,000 images.

01:47:13.659 --> 01:47:15.320
There was not one of —
Tim Ferriss: You took every image.

01:47:15.320 --> 01:47:16.320
Kevin Kelly: I took every image.

01:47:16.320 --> 01:47:17.320
Tim Ferriss: Wrote every caption.

01:47:17.320 --> 01:47:19.070
Kevin Kelly: I did all the layout.

01:47:19.070 --> 01:47:21.400
There's a thousand pages, each one has a different
design.

01:47:21.400 --> 01:47:23.190
I did the whole thing.

01:47:23.190 --> 01:47:27.599
And there's nobody who enjoys that book more
than me, just going through it.

01:47:27.599 --> 01:47:33.890
Of course, I was there, but I made up one
prototype book, like this book here is a prototype,

01:47:33.890 --> 01:47:36.510
and all I wanted was one book.

01:47:36.510 --> 01:47:40.440
If there was no other books made, I would've
been happy.

01:47:40.440 --> 01:47:43.360
But since I was making one, it is so easy
to make others.

01:47:43.360 --> 01:47:46.870
And so it's like, "If I can share it with
you," send it to my friends, that makes me

01:47:46.870 --> 01:47:48.000
even happier.

01:47:48.000 --> 01:47:50.700
So the success for that was having the book.

01:47:50.700 --> 01:47:54.370
This one is not so much for me.

01:47:54.370 --> 01:48:02.219
It is if other people also can gain and find
ways to repeat these things and improve their

01:48:02.219 --> 01:48:03.780
lives, that is success for me.

01:48:03.780 --> 01:48:09.780
So I would hope that a young person like me
would hear some of this advice and be able

01:48:09.780 --> 01:48:14.360
to encapsulate it and repeat it to themselves
and make their lives better.

01:48:14.360 --> 01:48:16.860
That would be a success for me.

01:48:16.860 --> 01:48:19.400
Tim Ferriss: I'm going to take a slight detour
because that is my wont.

01:48:19.400 --> 01:48:25.340
I'm going to take a slight detour because
this is on my mind.

01:48:25.340 --> 01:48:26.869
Do you still recommend sabbaticals?

01:48:26.869 --> 01:48:27.869
Kevin Kelly: Oh, gosh.

01:48:27.869 --> 01:48:34.969
Tim Ferriss: And how would you suggest, maybe
for people who have not heard our prior conversations,

01:48:34.969 --> 01:48:37.230
think about sabbaticals?

01:48:37.230 --> 01:48:39.830
The value of how to actually take a sabbatical.

01:48:39.830 --> 01:48:40.830
What does that mean?

01:48:40.830 --> 01:48:41.830
Kevin Kelly: Right, right.

01:48:41.830 --> 01:48:46.860
No, I'm a huge fan of sabbaticals and I think
I had some advice in the book.

01:48:46.860 --> 01:48:57.310
I put it that I think we over overemphasize
our productivity and efficiency, but the most

01:48:57.310 --> 01:49:02.920
powerful thing you can do for productivity
is to — the best thing for your work ethic

01:49:02.920 --> 01:49:04.130
is to have a rest ethic.

01:49:04.130 --> 01:49:05.130
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

01:49:05.130 --> 01:49:06.130
Actually, that's one I've started.

01:49:06.130 --> 01:49:07.130
Kevin Kelly: Right.

01:49:07.130 --> 01:49:11.250
Tim Ferriss: Something along the lines of
"The key to a great right work ethic is having

01:49:11.250 --> 01:49:12.570
a great rest ethic."

01:49:12.570 --> 01:49:20.760
Kevin Kelly: So this idea of, I think goofing
off, wasting time, sabbaticals, Sabbaths,

01:49:20.760 --> 01:49:26.409
taking a Sabbath, are all essential to the
creative life.

01:49:26.409 --> 01:49:29.369
They're absolutely, it's almost like sleep.

01:49:29.369 --> 01:49:31.260
You just have to do it.

01:49:31.260 --> 01:49:39.070
And it does it by rejuvenating you and shifting
your perspective by releasing you from — I

01:49:39.070 --> 01:49:42.160
mean, there's just so many things that it
works on.

01:49:42.160 --> 01:49:50.630
And most, I think for me, in my own experience,
the best things that I've done came after

01:49:50.630 --> 01:49:51.630
taking — shifting.

01:49:51.630 --> 01:49:52.739
It's the clutch.

01:49:52.739 --> 01:49:57.639
When you're shifting, you want to put the
clutch in, otherwise you just kind of grind.

01:49:57.639 --> 01:49:59.090
So I think it's really valuable.

01:49:59.090 --> 01:50:06.570
And again, not just every seven years, which
is a technical sabbatical, but like Sabbaths,

01:50:06.570 --> 01:50:12.719
vacations, but more importantly, time off
and goofing off.

01:50:12.719 --> 01:50:17.469
And I think I find that the young people,
believe it or not, the ones that I am associated

01:50:17.469 --> 01:50:18.719
with, don't goof off enough.

01:50:18.719 --> 01:50:24.330
They go right from college and their first
job and it's like, no.

01:50:24.330 --> 01:50:25.650
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:50:25.650 --> 01:50:26.980
Kevin Kelly: Go.

01:50:26.980 --> 01:50:31.429
There were people that Wired, they came right
from college and after seven years they were

01:50:31.429 --> 01:50:32.429
still there.

01:50:32.429 --> 01:50:37.449
I was like, I sit down, like "Why are you
here?

01:50:37.449 --> 01:50:38.739
You should not be here.

01:50:38.739 --> 01:50:42.020
I mean, when was the last time you goofed
off or wasted time?"

01:50:42.020 --> 01:50:43.020
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:50:43.020 --> 01:50:46.040
Kevin Kelly: So no, I'm a big believer.

01:50:46.040 --> 01:50:47.860
Tim Ferriss: I can imagine the rest of management
must have loved that.

01:50:47.860 --> 01:50:49.650
Kevin Kelly: I was the management.

01:50:49.650 --> 01:50:51.889
Tim Ferriss: No, were you the only management?

01:50:51.889 --> 01:50:53.460
Anyway, we don't have to belabor the point.

01:50:53.460 --> 01:50:56.130
I'm just management like "Why — wait, why
did seven people quit last week?"

01:50:56.130 --> 01:50:57.570
"Oh, they've been talking to that Kelly."

01:50:57.570 --> 01:51:00.540
Kevin Kelly: So I think it's essential.

01:51:00.540 --> 01:51:04.349
And so your question of, whoa — this is
why I love you.

01:51:04.349 --> 01:51:07.260
It's like, give me some practical stuff about
how to take a sabbatical.

01:51:07.260 --> 01:51:09.889
Tim Ferriss: Or just what might it look like?

01:51:09.889 --> 01:51:12.360
For someone who — I mean, let's personalize
it.

01:51:12.360 --> 01:51:13.360
Like for me —
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:51:13.360 --> 01:51:15.560
Tim Ferriss: — what might a sabbatical look
like?

01:51:15.560 --> 01:51:22.890
What would you consider the minimal viable
duration and what would make it a sabbatical,

01:51:22.890 --> 01:51:27.030
versus me just being in a foreign locale,
thinking about the usual stuff?

01:51:27.030 --> 01:51:33.159
Kevin Kelly: So my wife's, both of her companies
she'd been at, she was at Genentech for 30

01:51:33.159 --> 01:51:35.310
years, and she's at 23andMe now.

01:51:35.310 --> 01:51:40.679
Well, Genentech had a sabbatical program,
an official one, and it was six weeks every

01:51:40.679 --> 01:51:43.130
six years, which is basically a European vacation.

01:51:43.130 --> 01:51:44.150
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:51:44.150 --> 01:51:45.860
Kevin Kelly: An annual vacation.

01:51:45.860 --> 01:51:51.630
So I think six weeks is probably, to me, the
minimum for a sabbatical.

01:51:51.630 --> 01:51:55.929
But man, it could be very effective in six
weeks.

01:51:55.929 --> 01:51:59.070
For me, I've done things like doing art is
something I like.

01:51:59.070 --> 01:52:04.140
In the old days, you used to have — professors
would get sabbaticals and they would do their

01:52:04.140 --> 01:52:09.889
own project that they were working on, or
go to a school somewhere or have a visiting

01:52:09.889 --> 01:52:14.290
appointment.

01:52:14.290 --> 01:52:18.020
Travel is a very, very common one.

01:52:18.020 --> 01:52:26.300
For you, I think your effort right now to
work in animation and stuff — what's the

01:52:26.300 --> 01:52:27.300
word I want?

01:52:27.300 --> 01:52:28.300
Tim Ferriss: I could shadow.

01:52:28.300 --> 01:52:29.889
Kevin Kelly: So I guess the general pattern
is you want something that's different in

01:52:29.889 --> 01:52:32.850
the structure and rhythm from what you normally
do.

01:52:32.850 --> 01:52:38.989
And so our temptation, those of us who like
to make things, is to make something different.

01:52:38.989 --> 01:52:41.690
But that's not really a sabbatical.

01:52:41.690 --> 01:52:42.970
We're kind of in the same rhythm.

01:52:42.970 --> 01:52:43.970
We're learning.

01:52:43.970 --> 01:52:47.640
And so it's sort of like you want to go in
a different direction.

01:52:47.640 --> 01:52:52.170
And I did a sabbatical once where I just read
books and it was like literally, it's all

01:52:52.170 --> 01:52:53.170
I'm going to do.

01:52:53.170 --> 01:52:57.739
I'm going to get up in the morning and I'm
going to read books and I'm not going to do

01:52:57.739 --> 01:52:58.739
anything else.

01:52:58.739 --> 01:53:05.800
And that was really different from my normal
behavior, but incredibly powerful.

01:53:05.800 --> 01:53:10.060
And by the end, I went to travel 'cause I
had to, it was like I blew my gaskets, I had

01:53:10.060 --> 01:53:11.060
to go.

01:53:11.060 --> 01:53:16.909
But that sabbatical of only reading books
and reading books all day long.

01:53:16.909 --> 01:53:20.820
And I could read almost like about an average
of a book and a half, about a book a day.

01:53:20.820 --> 01:53:23.150
'Cause some books are really short and some
are really long.

01:53:23.150 --> 01:53:28.510
But I mean the synapse associations that you
get from reading a book after book after book,

01:53:28.510 --> 01:53:31.270
you begin to think that they're all talking
to each other, the authors.

01:53:31.270 --> 01:53:32.270
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:53:32.270 --> 01:53:34.390
Kevin Kelly: I mean they're talking about
the same thing, they must have known about

01:53:34.390 --> 01:53:35.390
each other.

01:53:35.390 --> 01:53:39.110
It's impossible, but you have this sense of
this all knitting together.

01:53:39.110 --> 01:53:40.110
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:53:40.110 --> 01:53:41.110
Saturation.

01:53:41.110 --> 01:53:42.110
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:53:42.110 --> 01:53:48.020
So I think the recipe is to have a different
rhythm and a different mode than you normally

01:53:48.020 --> 01:53:49.020
have.

01:53:49.020 --> 01:53:51.290
So I don't know, for you that might be something
like —

01:53:51.290 --> 01:53:54.829
Tim Ferriss: I'm also used to making, making,
making.

01:53:54.829 --> 01:53:55.829
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:53:55.829 --> 01:53:59.659
Tim Ferriss: It could be interesting for me
just to shadow somebody for a period of time.

01:53:59.659 --> 01:54:00.659
Kevin Kelly: Right.

01:54:00.659 --> 01:54:04.860
Tim Ferriss: Somebody who's really good in
the world of, say animation, using that example,

01:54:04.860 --> 01:54:06.489
and just watch.

01:54:06.489 --> 01:54:09.159
I'm not allowed, actually —
Kevin Kelly: There you go.

01:54:09.159 --> 01:54:10.159
I like that.

01:54:10.159 --> 01:54:11.159
Tim Ferriss: To make anything for a while.

01:54:11.159 --> 01:54:12.610
I just have to watch.

01:54:12.610 --> 01:54:14.090
That would actually be very challenging for
me.

01:54:14.090 --> 01:54:15.090
Kevin Kelly: It would be.

01:54:15.090 --> 01:54:16.090
That's right.

01:54:16.090 --> 01:54:17.090
Yeah.

01:54:17.090 --> 01:54:18.090
It's like you have to watch somebody.

01:54:18.090 --> 01:54:19.849
But I do that every evening on YouTube.

01:54:19.849 --> 01:54:24.730
And again, I could rant about YouTube forever
because I think it's way underrated for an

01:54:24.730 --> 01:54:25.730
influencer society.

01:54:25.730 --> 01:54:27.849
Tim Ferriss: I heard a story, I'm not going
to name names.

01:54:27.849 --> 01:54:28.849
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:54:28.849 --> 01:54:31.989
Tim Ferriss: Actually I can name one name
but I don't want to mention all the names.

01:54:31.989 --> 01:54:33.320
So mutual friend, Matt Mullenweg.

01:54:33.320 --> 01:54:34.320
Kevin Kelly: Right.

01:54:34.320 --> 01:54:37.710
Tim Ferriss: We were just spending time together
and he said you went on a walk with one of

01:54:37.710 --> 01:54:38.710
his friends.

01:54:38.710 --> 01:54:39.710
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:54:39.710 --> 01:54:42.050
Tim Ferriss: And the two of you just talked
about YouTube for two hours straight.

01:54:42.050 --> 01:54:43.230
Kevin Kelly: Yes, I know.

01:54:43.230 --> 01:54:45.329
Tim Ferriss: So why don't you expand a little
bit?

01:54:45.329 --> 01:54:46.750
All right, so what do you do on YouTube?

01:54:46.750 --> 01:54:48.090
Kevin Kelly: I watch, I watch people.

01:54:48.090 --> 01:54:49.090
No, no, no.

01:54:49.090 --> 01:54:50.090
I'm sorry.

01:54:50.090 --> 01:54:51.730
Tim Ferriss: "It's this thing called YouTube.

01:54:51.730 --> 01:54:52.730
It has videos, Tim."

01:54:52.730 --> 01:54:53.730
Yes.

01:54:53.730 --> 01:54:54.730
Kevin Kelly: No, I watch people work.

01:54:54.730 --> 01:54:55.730
Tim Ferriss: You watch people work?

01:54:55.730 --> 01:54:56.730
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

01:54:56.730 --> 01:54:57.730
I watch people making things.

01:54:57.730 --> 01:54:58.730
Tim Ferriss: Like what?

01:54:58.730 --> 01:55:05.790
Kevin Kelly: Like people restoring cars, people
making boats, people making clay things, just

01:55:05.790 --> 01:55:07.139
watching people make things.

01:55:07.139 --> 01:55:08.139
Tim Ferriss: Huh.

01:55:08.139 --> 01:55:13.060
Kevin Kelly: And because I'm a maker and because
I learned so much by watching people work.

01:55:13.060 --> 01:55:14.929
Much more than you ever learn in a book.

01:55:14.929 --> 01:55:20.810
Tim Ferriss: And are these just full capture
videos or are they giving commentary as they

01:55:20.810 --> 01:55:21.810
go?

01:55:21.810 --> 01:55:24.440
Or are they just a kind of a peek over the
shoulder as they do their thing?

01:55:24.440 --> 01:55:25.440
Kevin Kelly: All the above.

01:55:25.440 --> 01:55:26.770
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:55:26.770 --> 01:55:27.820
Kevin Kelly: All of the above.

01:55:27.820 --> 01:55:29.800
Sometimes they're time lapses, them just in
the shop.

01:55:29.800 --> 01:55:33.340
Sometimes they're giving lessons, sometimes
they're showing a technique, sometimes they're

01:55:33.340 --> 01:55:34.550
walking through it.

01:55:34.550 --> 01:55:36.449
Some record their mistakes, some don't.

01:55:36.449 --> 01:55:41.440
I mean, I subscribe to so many that it's hard
to generalize.

01:55:41.440 --> 01:55:47.480
But the point is that why I think it's underappreciated,
is that it's an accelerant on the learning,

01:55:47.480 --> 01:55:53.090
the process of people discovering something,
putting it up, other people, other makers

01:55:53.090 --> 01:55:54.090
watching it, seeing, "Oh, that's a good idea."

01:55:54.090 --> 01:55:55.090
Tim Ferriss: It's like your visual —
Kevin Kelly: Modifying it —

01:55:55.090 --> 01:55:56.469
Tim Ferriss: — your visual discord.

01:55:56.469 --> 01:56:02.900
Kevin Kelly: Modifying it, putting their version
up, and then two days later someone else has

01:56:02.900 --> 01:56:04.250
improved it.

01:56:04.250 --> 01:56:10.510
And brain surgeons are using this right now
where a brain surgeon will have an operation,

01:56:10.510 --> 01:56:14.390
they'll be filming their operation, they have
a little bit of a technique improvement that

01:56:14.390 --> 01:56:15.390
they'll watch.

01:56:15.390 --> 01:56:17.350
Other brain surgeons are watching brain surgeons
—

01:56:17.350 --> 01:56:18.350
Tim Ferriss: On YouTube?

01:56:18.350 --> 01:56:19.350
Kevin Kelly: On YouTube.

01:56:19.350 --> 01:56:21.179
And within days they'll have an improvement.

01:56:21.179 --> 01:56:25.599
And it's going this fast, unlike years and
years of waiting until a paper being published

01:56:25.599 --> 01:56:26.599
in a paper.

01:56:26.599 --> 01:56:28.099
It's like, how can you do that?

01:56:28.099 --> 01:56:31.770
So it's this incredible accelerant and the
problem — not the problem, but the thing

01:56:31.770 --> 01:56:33.280
about YouTube is it's all invisible.

01:56:33.280 --> 01:56:35.610
It's not like a bookstore where you see what's
there.

01:56:35.610 --> 01:56:37.219
You have no idea —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:56:37.219 --> 01:56:38.869
Kevin Kelly: — that this is happening.

01:56:38.869 --> 01:56:39.869
And it's tremendous.

01:56:39.869 --> 01:56:44.560
Tim Ferriss: It's like being in the libraries
of Alexandria with a blindfold on.

01:56:44.560 --> 01:56:45.560
Kevin Kelly: Exactly.

01:56:45.560 --> 01:56:47.480
Tim Ferriss: And you're only allowed to take
it off once you grab something off the shelf.

01:56:47.480 --> 01:56:48.710
Kevin Kelly: You have a little tiny peephole.

01:56:48.710 --> 01:56:51.060
Oh, there's some books there about astronomy.

01:56:51.060 --> 01:56:53.170
But no, there's this huge world.

01:56:53.170 --> 01:56:59.850
So for me — but anyway, I like your idea
of watching people execute at work and not

01:56:59.850 --> 01:57:01.160
having to produce something.

01:57:01.160 --> 01:57:03.750
That would be a tremendous sabbatical, or
true sabbatical.

01:57:03.750 --> 01:57:07.989
Tim Ferriss: It would also be such a shift
for maybe because so much of what I do is

01:57:07.989 --> 01:57:12.690
virtual or on a screen that doing it say via
YouTube would not be much of a behavioral

01:57:12.690 --> 01:57:13.690
shift.

01:57:13.690 --> 01:57:18.989
And doing it in person, I also travel and
move around so much.

01:57:18.989 --> 01:57:20.810
Being in, say, a fixed location for a period
of time —

01:57:20.810 --> 01:57:21.810
Kevin Kelly: Right, right.

01:57:21.810 --> 01:57:22.810
Tim Ferriss: — would be a very novel experience.

01:57:22.810 --> 01:57:23.810
Kevin Kelly: Exactly.

01:57:23.810 --> 01:57:24.810
Right.

01:57:24.810 --> 01:57:25.810
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:57:25.810 --> 01:57:26.940
Kevin Kelly: So I was thinking of the productivity.

01:57:26.940 --> 01:57:28.040
There's one little bit of advice.

01:57:28.040 --> 01:57:33.881
Actually, I was telling us to David Allen
of all people, who was the Getting Things

01:57:33.881 --> 01:57:34.881
Done guy.

01:57:34.881 --> 01:57:35.881
Tim Ferriss: Of course.

01:57:35.881 --> 01:57:38.551
Kevin Kelly: There is a tendency — what's
the word?

01:57:38.551 --> 01:57:43.389
The normal approach to organizing your life,
is that you want to be productive.

01:57:43.389 --> 01:57:47.679
So you want to get through the things that
you need done the most productive way in the

01:57:47.679 --> 01:57:49.530
least time as possible.

01:57:49.530 --> 01:57:54.889
But I find it's better to shift over to say,
what are the kinds of things I want to do

01:57:54.889 --> 01:57:58.300
that I want to spend as much time as possible
doing?

01:57:58.300 --> 01:58:04.159
That to me is the focus, is to shift from
minimizing the amount of time on things, but

01:58:04.159 --> 01:58:09.409
to maximize the amount of time to do things
that you don't ever want to stop.

01:58:09.409 --> 01:58:11.060
Tim Ferriss: Where you want to maximize the
time.

01:58:11.060 --> 01:58:12.060
Kevin Kelly: Right.

01:58:12.060 --> 01:58:15.310
Tim Ferriss: That was one, I'm not going to
go through all the pages to find it, but that

01:58:15.310 --> 01:58:20.070
was actually one I had my thumb on, literally,
that I was going to bring up because that

01:58:20.070 --> 01:58:22.929
is a piece of advice in the book.

01:58:22.929 --> 01:58:26.829
I have to ask because I've never been able
to figure it out, probably because I haven't

01:58:26.829 --> 01:58:28.310
asked you specifically.

01:58:28.310 --> 01:58:34.739
Why are you so — you're like the — David
Hasselhoff is to Germany, what you are to

01:58:34.739 --> 01:58:36.619
China, you're huge in China.

01:58:36.619 --> 01:58:38.780
What is the reason for that, do you think?

01:58:38.780 --> 01:58:39.940
Kevin Kelly: It was an accident.

01:58:39.940 --> 01:58:40.940
It was an accident.

01:58:40.940 --> 01:58:48.480
So I wrote a book, Out of Control, in the
early '90s, that was ignored in the US.

01:58:48.480 --> 01:58:49.619
It was way too early.

01:58:49.619 --> 01:58:50.699
It was just too early.

01:58:50.699 --> 01:58:57.820
It was talking about decentralized systems,
had a whole chapter on crypto in 1994, that

01:58:57.820 --> 01:59:01.320
actually I gave to Steven Levy and assigned
him to follow up.

01:59:01.320 --> 01:59:05.739
And he later wrote the book on the crypto
stuff.

01:59:05.739 --> 01:59:08.489
Crypto not being the money, but mine was about
digital money.

01:59:08.489 --> 01:59:11.619
And so it was just too early.

01:59:11.619 --> 01:59:19.030
But it was translated into Chinese at the
beginning of the aughts, I don't know, 2006,

01:59:19.030 --> 01:59:20.730
maybe five, I don't remember exactly.

01:59:20.730 --> 01:59:23.960
And it was actually crowdsourced translated,
which was even more interesting.

01:59:23.960 --> 01:59:27.250
Tim Ferriss: Okay, because there was so much
demand by the Chinese?

01:59:27.250 --> 01:59:28.540
Kevin Kelly: No.

01:59:28.540 --> 01:59:30.070
It was just some fans in China.

01:59:30.070 --> 01:59:31.239
Basically it was —
Tim Ferriss: That's a task.

01:59:31.239 --> 01:59:33.260
Kevin Kelly: It was crowdsourced.

01:59:33.260 --> 01:59:37.400
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I get it, but crowd — it's
still a task.

01:59:37.400 --> 01:59:42.329
Kevin Kelly: So there was one guy, and I owe
it all to one guy, who was a real true fan

01:59:42.329 --> 01:59:45.199
and he organized the crowdsourcing translation
of it.

01:59:45.199 --> 01:59:51.970
And it came out at the right moment that Jack
Ma and Pony Ma were beginning to build their

01:59:51.970 --> 01:59:53.270
— do their internet things.

01:59:53.270 --> 01:59:56.300
And it was hugely influential on them.

01:59:56.300 --> 01:59:59.079
And they talk about the book.

01:59:59.079 --> 02:00:04.510
So everybody else, every entrepreneur in China
has to read the book because Jack Ma recommended

02:00:04.510 --> 02:00:05.510
it.

02:00:05.510 --> 02:00:06.510
Tim Ferriss: Right.

02:00:06.510 --> 02:00:07.510
The Steve Jobs of China —
Kevin Kelly: Exactly.

02:00:07.510 --> 02:00:08.510
Tim Ferriss: — is talking about it.

02:00:08.510 --> 02:00:10.989
Kevin Kelly: And that's primarily why it kind
of disseminated from that.

02:00:10.989 --> 02:00:16.840
And then all my other things were translated
and I became the prophet of the internet or

02:00:16.840 --> 02:00:23.340
something because I was talking about these
things before there was the things.

02:00:23.340 --> 02:00:25.380
Tim Ferriss: Kind of what prophets do.

02:00:25.380 --> 02:00:30.010
Kevin Kelly: And so in China there is a little
bit of more of a herd mentality where people

02:00:30.010 --> 02:00:33.280
read it because other people have been reading
it and need to read it.

02:00:33.280 --> 02:00:34.329
Tim Ferriss: Happens here too.

02:00:34.329 --> 02:00:36.580
Kevin Kelly: But even to a larger —
Tim Ferriss: To a greater extent.

02:00:36.580 --> 02:00:41.159
Kevin Kelly: And so most of my fans are actually
in China.

02:00:41.159 --> 02:00:47.090
And I still was going there on a regular basis
giving talks about the future of X and Y.

02:00:47.090 --> 02:00:55.239
And the major difference there was that they
were actively listening and then executing

02:00:55.239 --> 02:00:59.940
and doing this stuff rather than — I mean,
they were so eager to build and to get ahead,

02:00:59.940 --> 02:01:04.120
they would say, we're talking about whatever
it is, blockchain, whatever, "Okay, we're

02:01:04.120 --> 02:01:05.120
going to start doing blockchain."

02:01:05.120 --> 02:01:09.219
It was like they were absorbing it and actually
acting upon it rather than just kind of, "Oh,

02:01:09.219 --> 02:01:11.280
that's a nice idea."

02:01:11.280 --> 02:01:13.550
They were really looking for things to do.

02:01:13.550 --> 02:01:15.040
Tim Ferriss: Very proactive.

02:01:15.040 --> 02:01:19.190
Kevin Kelly: So it had a huge influence in
that way.

02:01:19.190 --> 02:01:20.710
Tim Ferriss: So you said you're known as KK?

02:01:20.710 --> 02:01:21.710
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

02:01:21.710 --> 02:01:25.849
Tim Ferriss: Do they write it in the English
"KK," or is there — I'm sure they have a

02:01:25.849 --> 02:01:28.450
Chinese name for you, but —
Kevin Kelly: They say it — there isn't characters.

02:01:28.450 --> 02:01:31.219
Tim Ferriss: It's just the letter K, letter
K.

02:01:31.219 --> 02:01:33.159
Kevin Kelly: They can read the letters.

02:01:33.159 --> 02:01:34.159
Tim Ferriss: No, I'm sure they can.

02:01:34.159 --> 02:01:35.159
Kevin Kelly: No, no, but I mean, it's just
KK.

02:01:35.159 --> 02:01:36.159
I have a Chinese name.

02:01:36.159 --> 02:01:37.159
Tim Ferriss: What is your Chinese name?

02:01:37.159 --> 02:01:38.159
Kevin Kelly: Kǎiwén Kǎilì.

02:01:38.159 --> 02:01:39.949
Tim Ferriss: That is a great name.

02:01:39.949 --> 02:01:42.349
Kevin Kelly: Yeah.

02:01:42.349 --> 02:01:44.750
Tim Ferriss: That is a great name.

02:01:44.750 --> 02:01:46.889
Kevin Kelly: But —
Tim Ferriss: You could slightly change that

02:01:46.889 --> 02:01:49.489
and sell yourself as a K-pop star in the US.

02:01:49.489 --> 02:01:52.460
It's all the rage, you can do really well.

02:01:52.460 --> 02:01:53.460
[foreign language].

02:01:53.460 --> 02:01:54.460
Cool older brother.

02:01:54.460 --> 02:01:55.460
Kevin Kelly: Yeah, right.

02:01:55.460 --> 02:01:57.090
Tim Ferriss: That's you, man.

02:01:57.090 --> 02:01:59.710
That's how I think about you.

02:01:59.710 --> 02:02:04.349
Well, is there any other advice you'd like
to give or anything else you would like to

02:02:04.349 --> 02:02:11.949
say about Excellent Advice for Living: Wisdom
I Wish I'd Known Earlier?

02:02:11.949 --> 02:02:14.510
Or any other thoughts you would like to share?

02:02:14.510 --> 02:02:15.869
Any closing comments?

02:02:15.869 --> 02:02:16.869
Requests of my audience?

02:02:16.869 --> 02:02:17.869
Kevin Kelly: Yes.

02:02:17.869 --> 02:02:21.560
Tim Ferriss: Formal complaints you'd like
to lodge?

02:02:21.560 --> 02:02:22.560
Anything at all?

02:02:22.560 --> 02:02:23.560
Kevin Kelly: Let's see.

02:02:23.560 --> 02:02:31.170
No, I think there is one little piece of advice
at the very end, which maybe sums up the assignment,

02:02:31.170 --> 02:02:36.810
which is kind of your goal in life is to be
able to say, on the day before you die, that

02:02:36.810 --> 02:02:39.150
you have fully become yourself.

02:02:39.150 --> 02:02:44.679
So I really want to emphasize this idea of
fully becoming yourself and the difficulty

02:02:44.679 --> 02:02:51.719
and the challenge that is to discover what
that is, but how powerful that is.

02:02:51.719 --> 02:02:58.579
And that's true whether you're starting a
company, or becoming an artist, or a teacher,

02:02:58.579 --> 02:02:59.579
whatever it is.

02:02:59.579 --> 02:03:08.620
And the reason why I'm very pro on technology
is that I think it enables us, helps us, generally,

02:03:08.620 --> 02:03:09.780
to become more of ourselves.

02:03:09.780 --> 02:03:16.510
That we all have mixtures of talents in us
that actually need external tools to help

02:03:16.510 --> 02:03:18.380
us express things.

02:03:18.380 --> 02:03:24.800
And so I am interested in increasing that
pool of possible tools in the world so that

02:03:24.800 --> 02:03:31.260
all of us would have some chance to really
expressing our genius and fully becoming ourselves.

02:03:31.260 --> 02:03:38.610
And that includes having clean water and education
and access to transportation.

02:03:38.610 --> 02:03:42.400
Those are all the fundamental tools in addition
to the kind of high tech stuff.

02:03:42.400 --> 02:03:46.929
But I really do believe that all of us have
a unique genius.

02:03:46.929 --> 02:03:50.750
I mean, every evidence I've seen in the world,
and people around the world suggest that that's

02:03:50.750 --> 02:03:51.750
true.

02:03:51.750 --> 02:04:02.059
And so if I can at all unleash people to attempt
to fulfill their best self, to be more of

02:04:02.059 --> 02:04:07.800
their selves, to be fully them, then that
would be a success for me.

02:04:07.800 --> 02:04:11.360
Tim Ferriss: Kevin, it's so nice to spend
time with you.

02:04:11.360 --> 02:04:12.360
So much fun.

02:04:12.360 --> 02:04:13.360
Always is.

02:04:13.360 --> 02:04:14.360
Kevin Kelly: It always is, Tim.

02:04:14.360 --> 02:04:15.360
You make me happy.

02:04:15.360 --> 02:04:16.360
I just love your sincerity.

02:04:16.360 --> 02:04:17.360
Thank you.

02:04:17.360 --> 02:04:18.360
Tim Ferriss: You too, man.

